export const articles = [
  {
    slug: "themost-framework-application-structure",
    folder: "",
    title: "The @themost-framework Application Structure",
    summary: "Use @themost/test as a map for organizing a @themost-framework application, from configuration and models to routes, authentication, and the database.",
    category: "Application Architecture",
    readingTime: "7 min read",
    source: "themost-framework-application-structure.md",
    contentHtml: "<h1>The @themost-framework Application Structure</h1>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://github.com/themost-framework/test\"><code>@themost/test</code></a> package is more than a test database. It is a small, working <code>@themost-framework</code> application that shows how configuration, models, Express routes, authentication, and storage fit together.</p>\n<p>Its server directory follows a useful separation of responsibilities:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-text\">modules/test/server/\n├── app.js\n├── standalone.js\n├── config/\n│   ├── app.json\n│   ├── models/\n│   └── public.pem / private.key\n├── db/\n│   └── local.db\n├── models/\n├── routes/\n└── views/\n</code></pre><p>The exact contents can grow with an application, but the boundary between these areas is the important part.</p>\n<h2><code>app.js</code> is the composition root</h2>\n<p>The application starts in <code>server/app.js</code>. Its <code>getApplication()</code> function creates the Express instance and composes the framework services:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> app = <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">express</span>()\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> serviceApplication = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">ExpressDataApplication</span>(\n  path.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">resolve</span>(applicationDir, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;config&#x27;</span>),\n)\n\napp.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">use</span>(express.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">json</span>({ <span class=\"hljs-attr\">reviver</span>: dateReviver }))\napp.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">use</span>(serviceApplication.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">middleware</span>(app))\n</code></pre><p>This file is the composition root. It wires together the configuration directory, the data application, authentication, routes, metadata, error handling, and views. It should assemble the application rather than become the place where every model rule is implemented.</p>\n<p>The data middleware is especially important. It creates an <code>ExpressDataContext</code> for a request and exposes it as <code>req.context</code>, allowing routes and services to query models through the active configuration.</p>\n<h2>Configuration is the application contract</h2>\n<p>The <code>server/config</code> directory is where the application declares how it should operate. Its central <code>app.json</code> configuration connects model loading, database adapters, and runtime settings.</p>\n<p>A simplified configuration shape looks like this:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;adapterTypes&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Test Data Adapter&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;invariantName&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;sqlite&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;@themost/sqlite#SqliteAdapter&quot;</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;adapters&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;test&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;invariantName&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;test&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;default&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-literal\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">true</span></span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;options&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n        <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;database&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;./db/local.db&quot;</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;settings&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;api&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;origin&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;http://localhost:3000&quot;</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><p>The exact values can differ by version and environment, but the responsibilities remain recognizable:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>adapterTypes</code> maps an invariant name to an adapter implementation.</li>\n<li><code>adapters</code> defines named connections and their options.</li>\n<li><code>default</code> selects the connection used when no other adapter is requested.</li>\n<li><code>settings</code> contains application-level options such as API origin and authentication behavior.</li>\n<li>Model definitions are loaded from the configured schema location, normally <code>config/models</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This arrangement keeps deployment choices out of model files. A model can say that <code>Order</code> has a <code>customer</code> and an <code>orderStatus</code>; the configuration decides which database stores those entities.</p>\n<h2>Model schemas define the domain</h2>\n<p>The <code>config/models</code> directory contains JSON schema definitions such as <code>Order.json</code>, <code>Person.json</code>, <code>Product.json</code>, and <code>User.json</code>. These files describe the domain vocabulary:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Product&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;fields&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;id&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Counter&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;primary&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-literal\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">true</span></span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Text&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;nullable&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-literal\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">false</span></span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;price&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Number&quot;</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><p>Schemas can also define relationships, inheritance, views, privileges, constraints, and event listeners. This is where the model contract belongs. It is not an Express route and it is not a database migration script.</p>\n<p>The framework&#39;s schema loader reads these definitions and makes them available through <code>context.model(&#39;Product&#39;)</code>. The OData model builder can then expose the same definitions as entity types, entity sets, and metadata.</p>\n<h2><code>models</code> adds executable behavior</h2>\n<p>The <code>server/models</code> directory contains JavaScript model classes for behavior that is easier to express in code. In <code>@themost/test</code>, the model classes build a hierarchy such as:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-text\">Thing -&gt; Account -&gt; User\n</code></pre><p>A class can add functions, validation helpers, or specialized behavior while the JSON schema continues to describe fields and relationships:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\">@<span class=\"hljs-title class_\">EdmMapping</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">entityType</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;User&#x27;</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">class</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">User</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">extends</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_ inherited__\">Account</span> {\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">static</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">async</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">getMe</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">context</span>) {\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> context.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">model</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;User&#x27;</span>)\n      .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">where</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;name&#x27;</span>).<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">equal</span>(context.<span class=\"hljs-property\">user</span> &amp;&amp; context.<span class=\"hljs-property\">user</span>.<span class=\"hljs-property\">name</span>)\n      .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">getTypedItem</span>()\n  }\n}\n</code></pre><p>This gives the application two complementary modelling tools. JSON schemas are excellent for declarative metadata. Model classes are useful when a domain operation needs executable logic.</p>\n<h2><code>db</code> holds the test storage</h2>\n<p>The <code>server/db</code> directory contains the SQLite fixture used by the test application. The repository describes it as a CRM-style database with customers, products, offers, orders, and related objects.</p>\n<p>When <code>@themost/test</code> starts its built-in application, it copies the local database to a temporary location before using it. That keeps test runs isolated and prevents one test run from permanently changing the repository fixture.</p>\n<p>This is a good pattern for a learning environment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The schema is inspectable.</li>\n<li>The database contains realistic relationships.</li>\n<li>Each run can start from known data.</li>\n<li>The same application can be used by tests and API clients.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In a production application, the adapter options would normally point to a managed database or an environment-specific database location instead of a copied local fixture.</p>\n<h2><code>routes</code> owns HTTP behavior</h2>\n<p>The <code>server/routes</code> directory contains Express routers for concerns such as authentication, documentation, and the application home page. These routes use <code>req.context</code> when they need model data, but they do not need to know how a database connection was created.</p>\n<p>The test application also mounts the framework&#39;s <code>serviceRouter</code> under <code>/api/</code>. That router exposes model-driven operations and metadata through the OData service surface. Custom routes can still be added for workflows that need a response or process beyond ordinary entity operations.</p>\n<p>A useful route remains small:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\">router.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">get</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;/me&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">async</span> (req, res, next) =&gt; {\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">try</span> {\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> user = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">await</span> req.<span class=\"hljs-property\">context</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">model</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;User&#x27;</span>)\n      .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">where</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;name&#x27;</span>).<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">equal</span>(req.<span class=\"hljs-property\">context</span>.<span class=\"hljs-property\">user</span>.<span class=\"hljs-property\">name</span>)\n      .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">getItem</span>()\n\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> res.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">json</span>(user)\n  } <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">catch</span> (error) {\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">next</span>(error)\n  }\n})\n</code></pre><p>The route handles HTTP input and output. The model handles the data vocabulary and query behavior.</p>\n<h2>Authentication is an application service</h2>\n<p>The test server registers an <code>Authenticator</code> application service. Its configuration reads JWT key paths from the application settings, and its routes expose token and login behavior under <code>/auth/</code>.</p>\n<p>The authentication flow is separate from the data model, but it uses the model context to validate users and clients. Once Passport authenticates a request, <code>@themost/express</code> makes the authenticated user available through the request context.</p>\n<p>This matters for model privileges. An <code>Order</code> privilege such as <code>customer/user eq me()</code> can evaluate ownership using the same authenticated identity that the route and service layer use.</p>\n<p>Keep private keys and credentials outside source-controlled configuration. The test project includes key files because it is a self-contained learning and testing environment; a deployed application should load secrets securely.</p>\n<h2><code>standalone.js</code> is the launcher</h2>\n<p>The <code>server/standalone.js</code> file is a command-line entry point. It creates the application with <code>getApplication()</code>, applies options such as port, host, and API origin, and starts it with <code>serveApplication()</code>:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> app = <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">getApplication</span>(cwd)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> service = app.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">get</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;ExpressDataApplication&#x27;</span>)\nservice.<span class=\"hljs-property\">configuration</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">setSourceAt</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;settings/api/origin&#x27;</span>, origin)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">await</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">serveApplication</span>(app, port, host)\n</code></pre><p>Keeping the launcher separate from <code>app.js</code> makes the application easy to reuse. Tests can import <code>getApplication()</code> without starting a permanent process, while the command-line package can choose its own port and host.</p>\n<h2>A request through the structure</h2>\n<p>A request to the test API passes through the layers in a predictable order:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-text\">client request\n    |\n    v\nExpress routes and authentication\n    |\n    v\nExpressDataApplication middleware\n    |\n    v\nrequest-scoped ExpressDataContext\n    |\n    v\nmodel schema and model behavior\n    |\n    v\n@themost/query expression\n    |\n    v\nconfigured database adapter\n    |\n    v\nSQLite, PostgreSQL, MySQL, Oracle, or another provider\n</code></pre><p>The same model definition can therefore participate in HTTP APIs, background jobs, tests, privileges, listeners, and database operations without each layer inventing a separate representation.</p>\n<h2>A useful starting structure</h2>\n<p>When creating a new application, begin with the shape demonstrated by <code>@themost/test</code>:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-text\">server/\n├── app.js              # compose the application\n├── config/\n│   ├── app.json        # adapters and runtime settings\n│   └── models/         # domain schemas\n├── models/             # executable model behavior\n├── routes/             # HTTP-specific routes and services\n├── db/                 # local or test database fixtures\n└── views/              # server-rendered pages, if needed\n</code></pre><p>The most important habit is to keep configuration explicit. Put adapter selection, model loading, API origin, authentication settings, and environment-specific options in configuration. Put domain shape in schemas. Put executable domain behavior in model classes or listeners. Put HTTP behavior in routes and services.</p>\n<p>That separation is what makes <code>@themost/test</code> useful as a learning application: every concern has a visible home, but all of them meet through the same <code>ExpressDataApplication</code> and model context.</p>\n",
  },
  {
    slug: "themost-express",
    folder: "",
    title: "Getting Started with @themost/express",
    summary: "Connect @themost/data to an Express application with request contexts, model queries, and the built-in service router.",
    category: "Express Integration",
    readingTime: "5 min read",
    source: "themost-express.md",
    contentHtml: "<h1>Getting Started with @themost/express</h1>\n<p><code>@themost/express</code> connects the model-first data layer of <code>@themost/data</code> to an Express.js application. It gives each request a data context, makes your configured models available through <code>req.context</code>, and provides a router for exposing those models as an API.</p>\n<p>The result is a useful boundary between HTTP and the domain model:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-text\">HTTP request -&gt; ExpressDataApplication -&gt; DataContext -&gt; Model query\n</code></pre><p>Install the package alongside Express:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">npm install express @themost/express\n</code></pre><h2>Create a data application</h2>\n<p>The central class is <code>ExpressDataApplication</code>. It loads the application configuration and initializes the model builder and service infrastructure:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> express <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;express&#x27;</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> path <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;node:path&#x27;</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> { <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">ExpressDataApplication</span>, dateReviver } <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;@themost/express&#x27;</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> app = <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">express</span>()\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> dataApplication = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">ExpressDataApplication</span>(\n  path.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">resolve</span>(process.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">cwd</span>(), <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;config&#x27;</span>),\n)\n\napp.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">use</span>(express.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">json</span>({ <span class=\"hljs-attr\">reviver</span>: dateReviver }))\napp.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">use</span>(dataApplication.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">middleware</span>(app))\n</code></pre><p>The configuration path points to the directory containing the application configuration, adapters, and model schemas. You can also construct the application with a configuration object when that is more convenient for tests or small applications.</p>\n<h2>Request-scoped data contexts</h2>\n<p>After the middleware is registered, each request receives a <code>context</code> property:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\">app.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">get</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;/people&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">async</span> (req, res, next) =&gt; {\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">try</span> {\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> people = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">await</span> req.<span class=\"hljs-property\">context</span>\n      .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">model</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Person&#x27;</span>)\n      .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">select</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;id&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;name&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;email&#x27;</span>)\n      .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">orderBy</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;name&#x27;</span>)\n      .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">getItems</span>()\n\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> res.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">json</span>(people)\n  } <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">catch</span> (error) {\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">next</span>(error)\n  }\n})\n</code></pre><p><code>req.context</code> is an <code>ExpressDataContext</code>. It is connected to the current application configuration and is the normal entry point for model queries during a request. The middleware also finalizes the context when the response closes, helping release request-specific data resources consistently.</p>\n<p>This is the important difference from putting a shared database object directly on the Express application: the context belongs to the request, while the application holds the reusable configuration and services.</p>\n<h2>Use the built-in service router</h2>\n<p>When you want to expose your configured entity models through the framework&#39;s standard API, use <code>serviceRouter</code>:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> passport <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;passport&#x27;</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> { serviceRouter } <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;@themost/express&#x27;</span>\n\napp.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">use</span>(\n  <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;/api&#x27;</span>,\n  passport.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">authenticate</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;bearer&#x27;</span>, { <span class=\"hljs-attr\">session</span>: <span class=\"hljs-literal\">false</span> }),\n  serviceRouter,\n)\n</code></pre><p>The router provides common operations for entity sets, individual entities, navigation properties, functions, actions, and metadata. The exact access rules still come from your model configuration and authentication setup.</p>\n<p>A client can then make a request such as:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-text\">GET /api/People?$select=id,name,email\n</code></pre><p>The API is model-driven: <code>People</code> is resolved from the configured model definitions, and the query options are translated into the data layer rather than being manually converted into SQL inside a route.</p>\n<h2>Traditional Express routes still work</h2>\n<p>You do not have to use the service router for every endpoint. A regular route can use the same request context and add application-specific behavior:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\">app.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">post</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;/people&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">async</span> (req, res, next) =&gt; {\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">try</span> {\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> person = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">await</span> req.<span class=\"hljs-property\">context</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">model</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Person&#x27;</span>).<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">save</span>(req.<span class=\"hljs-property\">body</span>)\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> res.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">status</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">201</span>).<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">json</span>(person)\n  } <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">catch</span> (error) {\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">next</span>(error)\n  }\n})\n</code></pre><p>This gives you a practical choice. Use the service router for consistent model APIs, and use ordinary Express routes when an endpoint needs a custom workflow, response, or integration with another service.</p>\n<h2>Run work outside a request</h2>\n<p>Background jobs and command-line tasks do not have <code>req.context</code>. <code>ExpressDataApplication</code> can create a context directly, or execute a callback and finalize the context for you:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\">dataApplication.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">execute</span>(<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">async</span> (context, done) =&gt; {\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">try</span> {\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> latestPeople = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">await</span> context\n      .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">model</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Person&#x27;</span>)\n      .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">where</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;dateModified&#x27;</span>).<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">greaterThan</span>(<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">Date</span>(<span class=\"hljs-title class_\">Date</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">now</span>() - <span class=\"hljs-number\">86400000</span>))\n      .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">getItems</span>()\n\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">done</span>(<span class=\"hljs-literal\">null</span>, latestPeople)\n  } <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">catch</span> (error) {\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">done</span>(error)\n  }\n}, <span class=\"hljs-function\">(<span class=\"hljs-params\">error, value</span>) =&gt;</span> {\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">if</span> (error) {\n    <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">console</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">error</span>(error)\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span>\n  }\n\n  <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">console</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">log</span>(value)\n})\n</code></pre><p>For promise-based code, create a context directly and finalize it when the operation completes. The key rule is the same as with any data context: finish the context after the work is done.</p>\n<h2>Extend the application</h2>\n<p><code>ExpressDataApplication</code> supports application services and strategies. A service can add routes or coordinate a cross-cutting capability while still receiving the configured application instance:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">class</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">HealthService</span> {\n  <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">constructor</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">application</span>) {\n    application.<span class=\"hljs-property\">container</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">subscribe</span>(<span class=\"hljs-function\">(<span class=\"hljs-params\">container</span>) =&gt;</span> {\n      <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">if</span> (container) {\n        container.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">get</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;/health&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-function\">(<span class=\"hljs-params\">req, res</span>) =&gt;</span> {\n          res.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">json</span>({ <span class=\"hljs-attr\">status</span>: <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;ok&#x27;</span> })\n        })\n      }\n    })\n  }\n}\n\ndataApplication.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">useService</span>(<span class=\"hljs-title class_\">HealthService</span>)\n</code></pre><p>This keeps extension points inside the application container instead of coupling every feature to the top-level Express setup.</p>\n<h2>The Express boundary</h2>\n<p><code>@themost/express</code> is deliberately focused. Express remains responsible for HTTP concerns such as routing, authentication middleware, and response handling. <code>@themost/data</code> remains responsible for models, queries, validation, relationships, privileges, and persistence. <code>@themost/express</code> gives them a request-aware context and a standard API path between them.</p>\n<p>Start with <code>ExpressDataApplication</code>, register its middleware before routes that need data access, and use <code>req.context.model(&#39;ModelName&#39;)</code> inside those routes. From there, the same model definitions can serve custom Express endpoints, the built-in service router, background work, and tests.</p>\n<p>For the complete integration surface, explore the <a href=\"https://github.com/themost-framework/express\"><code>@themost/express</code> repository</a> and its <a href=\"https://github.com/themost-framework/express#usage\">usage examples</a>.</p>\n",
  },
  {
    slug: "database-agnostic-query",
    folder: "",
    title: "A Database-Agnostic Query with @themost/query",
    summary: "Build readable queries with JavaScript expressions and let @themost/query translate them for different database engines.",
    category: "Querying",
    readingTime: "5 min read",
    source: "database-agnostic-query.md",
    contentHtml: "<h1>A Database-Agnostic Query with @themost/query</h1>\n<p><code>@themost/query</code> is the database-agnostic query module behind <code>@themost/data</code>. It lets application code describe filtering, selection, sorting, joins, and paging without writing a database-specific SQL string for every operation.</p>\n<p>The package separates two jobs:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Build a query expression that describes what data is needed.</li>\n<li>Let a formatter or data adapter translate and execute that expression for the configured database.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>That separation is what makes the same query useful with SQLite, PostgreSQL, MySQL, SQL Server, Oracle, and other supported adapters.</p>\n<h2>Install the query module</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">npm install @themost/query\n</code></pre><p>Most application code reaches <code>@themost/query</code> through <code>@themost/data</code>, but the query package can also be used directly when building adapter-level tools or reusable query definitions.</p>\n<h2>Build a simple query</h2>\n<p>A <code>QueryExpression</code> can select fields, identify its source entity, sort the result, and limit the number of rows:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> { <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">QueryExpression</span>, <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">SqlFormatter</span> } <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;@themost/query&#x27;</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> query = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">QueryExpression</span>()\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">select</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;id&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;name&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;category&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;price&#x27;</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">from</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;ProductData&#x27;</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">orderBy</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;name&#x27;</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">take</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">25</span>)\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> sql = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">SqlFormatter</span>().<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">format</span>(query)\n</code></pre><p>The expression describes the operation. <code>SqlFormatter</code> turns it into SQL for inspection or for use by a compatible adapter. The application does not need to concatenate column names and values into a SQL string by hand.</p>\n<h2>Filter with a field expression</h2>\n<p>The fluent API supports comparison methods such as <code>equal</code>, <code>greaterThan</code>, <code>lowerThan</code>, <code>in</code>, and their related operators:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> query = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">QueryExpression</span>()\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">select</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;id&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;name&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;price&#x27;</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">from</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;ProductData&#x27;</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">where</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;category&#x27;</span>).<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">equal</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Laptops&#x27;</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">and</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;price&#x27;</span>).<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">greaterThan</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">500</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">orderByDescending</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;price&#x27;</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">take</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>)\n</code></pre><p>The query expresses a business condition: find laptop products that cost more than 500 and return the most expensive ten first. The adapter is responsible for producing the syntax required by its database engine.</p>\n<h2>Use JavaScript closures</h2>\n<p>One of the distinctive features of <code>@themost/query</code> is its closure-to-query support. Instead of writing every condition through string methods, you can describe it with a JavaScript expression:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> { <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">QueryEntity</span>, <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">QueryExpression</span>, <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">SqlFormatter</span> } <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;@themost/query&#x27;</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> products = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">QueryEntity</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;ProductData&#x27;</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> query = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">QueryExpression</span>()\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">select</span>(<span class=\"hljs-function\">(<span class=\"hljs-params\">product</span>) =&gt;</span> ({\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">id</span>: product.<span class=\"hljs-property\">id</span>,\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">name</span>: product.<span class=\"hljs-property\">name</span>,\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">price</span>: product.<span class=\"hljs-property\">price</span>,\n  }))\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">from</span>(products)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">where</span>(<span class=\"hljs-function\">(<span class=\"hljs-params\">product</span>) =&gt;</span> product.<span class=\"hljs-property\">price</span> &gt; <span class=\"hljs-number\">500</span> &amp;&amp; product.<span class=\"hljs-property\">price</span> &lt;= <span class=\"hljs-number\">1000</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">orderBy</span>(<span class=\"hljs-function\">(<span class=\"hljs-params\">product</span>) =&gt;</span> product.<span class=\"hljs-property\">price</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">take</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>)\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> sql = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">SqlFormatter</span>().<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">format</span>(query)\n</code></pre><p>The closure is not executed against an in-memory array. The query parser reads the expression and produces an equivalent query expression, which can then be formatted for the target data provider. This gives developers a familiar JavaScript style while keeping execution in the database.</p>\n<h2>Select from a named entity</h2>\n<p><code>QueryEntity</code> represents the source entity in a query and can provide an alias:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> orders = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">QueryEntity</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;OrderData&#x27;</span>).<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">as</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;orders&#x27;</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> products = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">QueryEntity</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;ProductData&#x27;</span>).<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">as</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;orderedItem&#x27;</span>)\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> query = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">QueryExpression</span>()\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">select</span>(<span class=\"hljs-function\">(<span class=\"hljs-params\">order</span>) =&gt;</span> ({\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">id</span>: order.<span class=\"hljs-property\">id</span>,\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">orderDate</span>: order.<span class=\"hljs-property\">orderDate</span>,\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">product</span>: order.<span class=\"hljs-property\">orderedItem</span>.<span class=\"hljs-property\">name</span>,\n  }))\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">from</span>(orders)\n</code></pre><p>Aliases make joined and relational queries easier to read. They also keep generated names unambiguous when two entities contain fields with the same name.</p>\n<h2>Join related entities</h2>\n<p>A join combines rows from related entities. <code>@themost/query</code> supports inner joins as well as left and right joins:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> orders = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">QueryEntity</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;OrderData&#x27;</span>).<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">as</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;orders&#x27;</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> products = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">QueryEntity</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;ProductData&#x27;</span>).<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">as</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;orderedItem&#x27;</span>)\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> query = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">QueryExpression</span>()\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">select</span>(<span class=\"hljs-function\">(<span class=\"hljs-params\">order</span>) =&gt;</span> ({\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">id</span>: order.<span class=\"hljs-property\">id</span>,\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">orderDate</span>: order.<span class=\"hljs-property\">orderDate</span>,\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">productName</span>: order.<span class=\"hljs-property\">orderedItem</span>.<span class=\"hljs-property\">name</span>,\n  }))\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">from</span>(orders)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">join</span>(products)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">with</span>(<span class=\"hljs-function\">(<span class=\"hljs-params\">order, product</span>) =&gt;</span> order.<span class=\"hljs-property\">orderedItem</span> === product.<span class=\"hljs-property\">id</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">where</span>(<span class=\"hljs-function\">(<span class=\"hljs-params\">order</span>) =&gt;</span> order.<span class=\"hljs-property\">orderedItem</span>.<span class=\"hljs-property\">category</span> === <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Laptops&#x27;</span>)\n</code></pre><p>The join condition describes how the order and product are related. A database adapter can translate the expression into its own SQL dialect while preserving the relationship and filter.</p>\n<h2>Page and sort results</h2>\n<p>Use <code>take</code> and <code>skip</code> to create a page of results:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> query = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">QueryExpression</span>()\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">select</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;id&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;name&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;price&#x27;</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">from</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;ProductData&#x27;</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">orderBy</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;name&#x27;</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">take</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">25</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">skip</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">25</span>)\n</code></pre><p>This requests the second page when each page contains 25 records. Always pair paging with a stable order when the result is shown to a user. Without ordering, the database is free to return rows in an order that may change between requests.</p>\n<h2>Aggregate and group</h2>\n<p>The query language also supports aggregate functions such as <code>count</code>, <code>sum</code>, <code>avg</code>, <code>min</code>, and <code>max</code>:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> { <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">QueryEntity</span>, <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">QueryExpression</span>, <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">SqlFormatter</span>, count } <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;@themost/query&#x27;</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> products = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">QueryEntity</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;ProductData&#x27;</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> query = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">QueryExpression</span>()\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">select</span>(<span class=\"hljs-function\">(<span class=\"hljs-params\">product</span>) =&gt;</span> ({\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">category</span>: product.<span class=\"hljs-property\">category</span>,\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">total</span>: <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">count</span>(product.<span class=\"hljs-property\">id</span>),\n  }))\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">from</span>(products)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">groupBy</span>(<span class=\"hljs-function\">(<span class=\"hljs-params\">product</span>) =&gt;</span> product.<span class=\"hljs-property\">category</span>)\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> sql = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">SqlFormatter</span>().<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">format</span>(query)\n</code></pre><p>The result is a summary by category rather than one row for every product. Because the aggregation is part of the query expression, the database can perform the counting instead of the application loading every row first.</p>\n<h2>Query is not execution</h2>\n<p><code>@themost/query</code> builds and formats expressions. It does not need to own the application connection. A data adapter can execute the resulting expression:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> results = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">await</span> db.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">executeAsync</span>(query)\n</code></pre><p>In a model-driven application, <code>@themost/data</code> normally creates these query expressions for you when you call methods such as <code>context.model(&#39;Product&#39;).where(...)</code>. Understanding <code>@themost/query</code> helps explain what happens underneath that fluent model API.</p>\n<h2>Why database agnostic matters</h2>\n<p>A database-agnostic query is not a promise that every database has identical capabilities. It is a stable application vocabulary for common operations, with the provider-specific translation kept at the edge.</p>\n<p>That gives teams a few practical benefits:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Model and service code is less tied to one vendor&#39;s SQL dialect.</li>\n<li>Query intent is easier to read and test.</li>\n<li>Parameters can be handled by the query system instead of string concatenation.</li>\n<li>Adapters can optimize or format the same expression for different engines.</li>\n<li><code>@themost/data</code> can build model queries on top of a shared query language.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Start with <code>select</code>, <code>from</code>, <code>where</code>, <code>orderBy</code>, <code>take</code>, and <code>skip</code>. Add joins and aggregates when the domain needs them. The main habit is to describe the data operation as an expression and let the configured adapter handle the database-specific execution.</p>\n<p>For more examples, explore the <a href=\"https://github.com/themost-framework/query\"><code>@themost/query</code> repository</a> and the <a href=\"https://github.com/themost-framework/query#usage\">query module README</a>.</p>\n",
  },
  {
    slug: "data-dialects-and-adapters",
    folder: "",
    title: "Data Dialects and Database Adapters",
    summary: "Understand how @themost-framework keeps model code portable while @themost/mysql, @themost/sqlite, @themost/pg, @themost/oracle, and other adapters handle database-specific behavior.",
    category: "Data Layer",
    readingTime: "6 min read",
    source: "data-dialects-and-adapters.md",
    contentHtml: "<h1>Data Dialects and Database Adapters</h1>\n<p>A model-driven application should not need a different business layer for every database engine. <code>@themost-framework</code> keeps models and queries on a shared API, then delegates storage details to a database adapter.</p>\n<p>This is the role of packages such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>@themost/sqlite</code> for SQLite</li>\n<li><code>@themost/mysql</code> for MySQL and MariaDB</li>\n<li><code>@themost/pg</code> for PostgreSQL</li>\n<li><code>@themost/oracle</code> for Oracle Database</li>\n<li><code>@themost/mssql</code> for Microsoft SQL Server</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Together with <code>@themost/query</code>, these adapters form a dialect boundary. The application describes a query once, and the selected adapter translates it into the syntax and execution behavior required by its database.</p>\n<h2>The shared application layer</h2>\n<p>Application code uses <code>@themost/data</code> models and query methods instead of opening a provider-specific connection in every service:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> products = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">await</span> context\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">model</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Product&#x27;</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">where</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;category&#x27;</span>).<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">equal</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Laptops&#x27;</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">where</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;price&#x27;</span>).<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">greaterThan</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">500</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">orderBy</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;name&#x27;</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">take</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">25</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">getItems</span>()\n</code></pre><p>This query expresses domain intent. It does not mention SQLite placeholders, PostgreSQL casts, MySQL identifier rules, Oracle pagination syntax, or SQL Server&#39;s paging form. The active data adapter receives the resulting query expression and handles those details.</p>\n<p>The model layer also keeps field types, relationships, constraints, privileges, and listeners consistent when the storage provider changes.</p>\n<h2>Configure an adapter type</h2>\n<p>A data configuration registers the adapter implementation by a stable invariant name:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;adapterTypes&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;SQLite Data Adapter&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;invariantName&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;sqlite&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;@themost/sqlite#SqliteAdapter&quot;</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><p>The <code>type</code> identifies the package and exported adapter class. The <code>invariantName</code> is the configuration key used by an adapter definition. This lets the rest of the application refer to a provider consistently without importing its implementation into every model or controller.</p>\n<h2>Select the active connection</h2>\n<p>An adapter definition provides the connection options and can be marked as the default:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;adapters&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;development&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;invariantName&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;sqlite&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;default&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-literal\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">true</span></span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;options&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n        <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;database&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;./db/local.db&quot;</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><p>The options depend on the provider. A SQLite adapter may need a database file path. A server database adapter commonly needs a host, port, database name, user, password, and pool settings.</p>\n<p>For example, a PostgreSQL deployment might use a configuration shaped like this:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;adapters&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;production&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;invariantName&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;postgres&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;default&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-literal\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">true</span></span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;options&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n        <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;host&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;db.example.com&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n        <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;port&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">5432</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n        <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;database&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;catalog&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n        <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;user&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;app_user&quot;</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><p>Credentials should come from environment or secret configuration in a real deployment. They should not be committed to a model or source file.</p>\n<h2>Provider packages and dialect details</h2>\n<p>Each adapter is responsible for the behavior that cannot be made identical across databases. This may include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Quoting identifiers and reserved words</li>\n<li>Parameter placeholders and value binding</li>\n<li>Date and time functions</li>\n<li>Boolean and numeric representations</li>\n<li>Auto-increment or counter columns</li>\n<li>Pagination syntax</li>\n<li>Transaction and connection-pool behavior</li>\n<li>Native data types and provider-specific features</li>\n</ul>\n<p>That does not mean every database has exactly the same capabilities. It means the framework gives common operations a shared representation and leaves provider-specific translation at the adapter boundary.</p>\n<p>For example, the same query can use <code>take(25)</code> and <code>skip(25)</code> across providers. The adapter can format that paging request according to the selected database instead of forcing model code to know whether the target engine expects <code>LIMIT</code>, <code>OFFSET</code>, <code>FETCH</code>, or another form.</p>\n<h2>Query expressions are portable too</h2>\n<p><code>@themost/query</code> builds database-independent query expressions before an adapter formats or executes them:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> { <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">QueryEntity</span>, <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">QueryExpression</span>, <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">SqlFormatter</span> } <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;@themost/query&#x27;</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> orders = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">QueryEntity</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;OrderData&#x27;</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> query = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">QueryExpression</span>()\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">select</span>(<span class=\"hljs-function\">(<span class=\"hljs-params\">order</span>) =&gt;</span> ({\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">id</span>: order.<span class=\"hljs-property\">id</span>,\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">orderDate</span>: order.<span class=\"hljs-property\">orderDate</span>,\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">status</span>: order.<span class=\"hljs-property\">orderStatus</span>,\n  }))\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">from</span>(orders)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">where</span>(<span class=\"hljs-function\">(<span class=\"hljs-params\">order</span>) =&gt;</span> order.<span class=\"hljs-property\">orderStatus</span> === <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">orderByDescending</span>(<span class=\"hljs-function\">(<span class=\"hljs-params\">order</span>) =&gt;</span> order.<span class=\"hljs-property\">orderDate</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">take</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">20</span>)\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> sql = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">SqlFormatter</span>().<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">format</span>(query)\n</code></pre><p>The formatter is useful for inspecting the generated SQL, while a configured adapter can execute the expression directly. <code>@themost/data</code> usually hides this lower-level step behind its model query API.</p>\n<h2>Moving from SQLite to PostgreSQL</h2>\n<p>SQLite is convenient for local development and tests because it is file-based and easy to start. A production system may use PostgreSQL, MySQL, Oracle, or SQL Server for operational, scaling, or organizational reasons.</p>\n<p>A provider change usually involves:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Installing the matching adapter package.</li>\n<li>Registering its adapter type and invariant name.</li>\n<li>Changing the active adapter options.</li>\n<li>Migrating or verifying the database schema and data.</li>\n<li>Running the application&#39;s model and integration tests against the new provider.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The model definitions and normal query calls should remain the same. If a change requires provider-specific logic in a model or business service, isolate it behind a deliberate adapter or service boundary rather than spreading it through the application.</p>\n<h2>Test portability honestly</h2>\n<p>Database agnostic does not mean database independent in every edge case. A query that uses a common filter and sort is likely to travel well. A query that depends on a vendor-specific function, collation, JSON behavior, or transaction feature may need a provider-specific capability check.</p>\n<p>Use a small compatibility test suite for the operations your application relies on:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Create and update model entities.</li>\n<li>Query primitive fields and relationships.</li>\n<li>Apply paging and ordering.</li>\n<li>Execute transactions and rollbacks.</li>\n<li>Validate dates, counters, null values, and unique constraints.</li>\n<li>Exercise listeners, privileges, and calculated fields.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The same model tests can run against multiple adapters. That is one of the best ways to find a portability problem while it is still local and easy to fix.</p>\n<h2>Keep the dialect at the edge</h2>\n<p>The useful design boundary is simple: domain code talks in models and query expressions, while adapters talk in database dialects. <code>@themost/sqlite</code>, <code>@themost/mysql</code>, <code>@themost/pg</code>, <code>@themost/oracle</code>, and <code>@themost/mssql</code> make that boundary concrete for their respective engines.</p>\n<p>Choose the provider that fits the deployment, configure it explicitly, and keep the application vocabulary on <code>@themost/data</code> and <code>@themost/query</code>. That way, changing the database is a controlled infrastructure decision rather than a rewrite of the business model.</p>\n<p>Explore the adapter packages through the <a href=\"https://github.com/themost-framework\"><code>@themost-framework</code> GitHub organization</a> and the <a href=\"https://github.com/themost-framework/query\"><code>@themost/query</code> documentation</a>.</p>\n",
  },
  {
    slug: "connection-and-data-context",
    folder: "",
    title: "Connection and Data Context",
    summary: "Understand the DataContext lifecycle and why every database operation starts from a managed connection scope.",
    category: "Core Data Layer",
    readingTime: "4 min read",
    source: "connection-and-data-context.md",
    contentHtml: "<h1>Connection and Data Context</h1>\n<p>Every database operation in <code>@themost/data</code> requires an active <code>DataContext</code>. This object manages your database connection lifecycle and acts as the central gateway to your application models.</p>\n<p>To prevent memory issues or hung socket connections, a context must always be finalized at the end of its operational execution loop.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> { <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">FreedomDataContext</span> } <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;@themost/data&#x27;</span>;\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">async</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">function</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">connectToDatabase</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\"></span>) {\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> context = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">FreedomDataContext</span>();\n\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">try</span> {\n    <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">console</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">log</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Database context initialized successfully!&#x27;</span>);\n  } <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">catch</span> (error) {\n    <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">console</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">error</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Connection scope error:&#x27;</span>, error.<span class=\"hljs-property\">message</span>);\n  } <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">finally</span> {\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">await</span> context.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">finalizeAsync</span>();\n  }\n}\n</code></pre><h2>Why it matters</h2>\n<p>The <code>DataContext</code> is the runtime boundary for your data access. It is responsible for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>opening and managing the database connection</li>\n<li>exposing model references and query APIs</li>\n<li>coordinating save and transaction behavior</li>\n<li>releasing resources cleanly when work completes</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In short, the context is the foundation on which all data operations are built.</p>\n",
  },
  {
    slug: "basic-data-retrieval",
    folder: "",
    title: "Basic Data Retrieval",
    summary: "Use the fluent query API to fetch, filter, sort, and shape records with readable JavaScript patterns.",
    category: "Querying",
    readingTime: "5 min read",
    source: "basic-data-retrieval.md",
    contentHtml: "<h1>Basic Data Retrieval</h1>\n<p>Data fetching is initiated by calling <code>.asQueryable()</code> on any registered data model reference. <code>@themost/data</code> exposes a powerful, type-agnostic fluent API that resembles LINQ query patterns.</p>\n<p>This allows developers to chain clear modifiers like <code>.where()</code>, <code>.select()</code>, and <code>.orderBy()</code> to dynamically formulate execution logic.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">async</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">function</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">fetchActiveUsers</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">context</span>) {\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> users = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">await</span> context.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">model</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;User&#x27;</span>)\n    .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">asQueryable</span>()\n    .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">where</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;isActive&#x27;</span>).<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">equal</span>(<span class=\"hljs-literal\">true</span>)\n    .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">orderBy</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;lastName&#x27;</span>)\n    .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">select</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;id&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;username&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;email&#x27;</span>)\n    .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">getItems</span>();\n\n  <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">console</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">log</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Active Profiles:&#x27;</span>, users);\n}\n</code></pre><h2>Query fluency</h2>\n<p>The fluent API keeps the intent readable and close to the business logic. Instead of mixing SQL strings across the application, you express operations as a pipeline:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>filter rows</li>\n<li>add selection criteria</li>\n<li>order results</li>\n<li>fetch only what you need</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This is one of the main reasons the framework feels maintainable at scale.</p>\n",
  },
  {
    slug: "javascript-closures-for-filtering",
    folder: "",
    title: "JavaScript Closures for Filtering",
    summary: "Use native JavaScript predicates inside queries to express secure, readable filters without writing manual SQL.",
    category: "Querying",
    readingTime: "4 min read",
    source: "javascript-closures-for-filtering.md",
    contentHtml: "<h1>JavaScript Closures for Filtering</h1>\n<p>A standout feature of <code>@themost/data</code> is its ability to parse native JavaScript arrow functions inside a <code>.where()</code> constraint. The runtime analyzes the closure and converts it into a parameterized query plan.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">async</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">function</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">filterWithClosures</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">context</span>) {\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> premiumArticles = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">await</span> context.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">model</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Post&#x27;</span>)\n    .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">asQueryable</span>()\n    .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">where</span>(<span class=\"hljs-function\"><span class=\"hljs-params\">x</span> =&gt;</span> x.<span class=\"hljs-property\">isPremium</span> === <span class=\"hljs-literal\">true</span> &amp;&amp; x.<span class=\"hljs-property\">views</span> &gt; <span class=\"hljs-number\">1000</span> &amp;&amp; x.<span class=\"hljs-property\">title</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">includes</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Node&#x27;</span>))\n    .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">getItems</span>();\n\n  <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">console</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">log</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Filtered Premium Posts:&#x27;</span>, premiumArticles);\n}\n</code></pre><h2>Why closures are useful</h2>\n<p>This pattern keeps filtering logic close to the model and easy to read. It preserves the developer experience of writing plain JavaScript while still letting the framework translate the expression to a secure underlying query.</p>\n<p>This is a strong example of the model-first design: behavior is explicit, but storage details remain abstracted.</p>\n",
  },
  {
    slug: "relationships-and-eager-loading",
    folder: "",
    title: "Relationships and Eager Loading",
    summary: "Load related records intentionally with expansion rules that keep data retrieval efficient and expressive.",
    category: "Relationships",
    readingTime: "5 min read",
    source: "relationships-and-eager-loading.md",
    contentHtml: "<h1>Relationships and Eager Loading</h1>\n<p>To balance network optimization with fast lookups, foreign key model relationships are evaluated lazily. When a record needs to load together with its parent or nested collections, it must be declared via the <code>.expand()</code> expression method.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">async</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">function</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">getPostsWithAuthors</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">context</span>) {\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> articles = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">await</span> context.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">model</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Post&#x27;</span>)\n    .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">asQueryable</span>()\n    .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">where</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;published&#x27;</span>).<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">equal</span>(<span class=\"hljs-literal\">true</span>)\n    .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">expand</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;author&#x27;</span>)\n    .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">getItems</span>();\n\n  <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">console</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">dir</span>(articles, { <span class=\"hljs-attr\">depth</span>: <span class=\"hljs-literal\">null</span> });\n}\n</code></pre><h2>The value of expansion</h2>\n<p>Expansion lets the application request related entities in the same data fetch. This keeps the result set coherent and reduces the need for a second query cycle.</p>\n<p>For domain-heavy apps, this is a huge win: it preserves object relationships while keeping reads efficient and predictable.</p>\n",
  },
  {
    slug: "atomic-transactions",
    folder: "",
    title: "Atomic Transactions",
    summary: "Protect dependent data updates with transaction boundaries that roll back cleanly on failure.",
    category: "Data Integrity",
    readingTime: "4 min read",
    source: "atomic-transactions.md",
    contentHtml: "<h1>Atomic Transactions</h1>\n<p>When implementing multi-step mutation pipelines where multiple writes are dependent, wrapping instructions inside a structured transaction block is mandatory. The framework-level <code>executeInTransaction</code> loop guarantees automated rollback if any child operation fails.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">async</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">function</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">processOrderTransaction</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">context, orderPayload, itemPayload</span>) {\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">try</span> {\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">await</span> context.<span class=\"hljs-property\">db</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">executeInTransaction</span>(<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">async</span> () =&gt; {\n      <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">await</span> context.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">model</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Order&#x27;</span>).<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">save</span>(orderPayload);\n      <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">await</span> context.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">model</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;OrderItem&#x27;</span>).<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">save</span>(itemPayload);\n    });\n\n    <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">console</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">log</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Transaction safely committed to the database!&#x27;</span>);\n  } <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">catch</span> (error) {\n    <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">console</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">error</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Transaction failed. Database rolled back cleanly:&#x27;</span>, error.<span class=\"hljs-property\">message</span>);\n  }\n}\n</code></pre><h2>Why transactions matter</h2>\n<p>Transactions preserve consistency across dependent updates. If one save fails, the earlier state is rolled back so the system does not end up half-written.</p>\n<p>For business-critical flows such as order processing, payments, or record migrations, this is essential.</p>\n",
  },
  {
    slug: "data-listeners",
    folder: "",
    title: "Data Listeners",
    summary: "Intercept entity flows before and after persistence to validate state and add business behavior cleanly.",
    category: "Event Model",
    readingTime: "4 min read",
    source: "data-listeners.md",
    contentHtml: "<h1>Data Listeners</h1>\n<p>Data listeners intercept entity mutation lifecycles right before or directly after records reach persistence. This makes it possible to validate input, override values, or trigger side effects during the save pipeline.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">export</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">function</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">beforeSave</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">event, callback</span>) {\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> target = event.<span class=\"hljs-property\">target</span>;\n\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">if</span> (target.<span class=\"hljs-property\">email</span> &amp;&amp; target.<span class=\"hljs-property\">email</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">endsWith</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;@dispostable.com&#x27;</span>)) {\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">callback</span>(<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">Error</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Temporary email domains are strictly blocked.&#x27;</span>));\n  }\n\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">callback</span>();\n}\n</code></pre><h2>Practical value</h2>\n<p>Listeners provide a clean place for validation and cross-cutting behavior without coupling business logic to a storage implementation. They also help keep domain rules centralized rather than scattered across controllers or service methods.</p>\n",
  },
  {
    slug: "aggregations-and-grouping",
    folder: "",
    title: "Aggregations and Grouping",
    summary: "Summarize datasets with grouping and aggregate expressions that remain readable and portable.",
    category: "Analytics",
    readingTime: "5 min read",
    source: "aggregations-and-grouping.md",
    contentHtml: "<h1>Aggregations and Grouping</h1>\n<p>When combining operational vectors for counting, summary totals, or evaluating arithmetic averages, <code>@themost/data</code> handles backend groupings with a readable query pattern. The framework binds analytical projection clauses directly into JavaScript expressions.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">async</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">function</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">getAuthorStatsReport</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">context</span>) {\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> stats = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">await</span> context.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">model</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Post&#x27;</span>)\n    .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">asQueryable</span>()\n    .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">groupBy</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;authorId&#x27;</span>)\n    .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">select</span>(\n      <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;authorId&#x27;</span>,\n      <span class=\"hljs-function\"><span class=\"hljs-params\">x</span> =&gt;</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">Object</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">assign</span>(x, {\n        <span class=\"hljs-attr\">totalViews</span>: minxs.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">sum</span>(x.<span class=\"hljs-property\">views</span>),\n        <span class=\"hljs-attr\">articleCount</span>: minxs.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">count</span>(x.<span class=\"hljs-property\">id</span>)\n      })\n    )\n    .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">getItems</span>();\n\n  <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">console</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">log</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Author Statistics:&#x27;</span>, stats);\n}\n</code></pre><h2>Why it matters</h2>\n<p>Analytics use cases often require summary data, not just record-level retrieval. Grouping and aggregation let the application compute business signals without writing custom database-specific SQL paths for every report.</p>\n",
  },
  {
    slug: "schema-definition-and-basic-attributes",
    folder: "",
    title: "Schema Definition and Basic Attributes",
    summary: "Declare models with schema metadata that defines storage mapping, constraints, and field behavior.",
    category: "Schema Design",
    readingTime: "5 min read",
    source: "schema-definition-and-basic-attributes.md",
    contentHtml: "<h1>Schema Definition and Basic Attributes</h1>\n<p>Model properties in <code>@themost/data</code> are declared using JSON-based schema configuration. These files define storage source locations, field types, sizes, indexing options, and required constraints.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Product&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;id&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">101</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;source&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Products&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;view&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ProductsView&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;fields&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;id&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Counter&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;primary&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-literal\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">true</span></span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;sku&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Text&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;size&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">50</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;nullable&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-literal\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">false</span></span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;unique&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-literal\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">true</span></span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;price&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Currency&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;nullable&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-literal\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">false</span></span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><h2>Why schema metadata matters</h2>\n<p>Schemas make the storage contract visible and maintainable. They give the framework a consistent description of the domain while allowing different backends to implement that contract in their own way.</p>\n",
  },
  {
    slug: "model-associations",
    folder: "",
    title: "Model Associations",
    summary: "Model one-to-many and many-to-many relationships with declarative field configuration and implicit joins.",
    category: "Relations",
    readingTime: "5 min read",
    source: "model-associations.md",
    contentHtml: "<h1>Model Associations</h1>\n<p>Declaring relational architecture fields is done within the schema definition by matching a field <code>type</code> to a target model object token. This can represent a single foreign reference or a many-to-many collection through an implicit junction relationship.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Order&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;id&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">102</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;source&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Orders&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;fields&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;id&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Counter&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;primary&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-literal\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">true</span></span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;customer&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;User&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;nullable&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-literal\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">false</span></span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;description&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Establishes a many-to-one foreign key mapping to the User model&quot;</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;tags&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Tag&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;many&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-literal\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">true</span></span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;description&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Creates an implicit many-to-many junction relationship table&quot;</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><h2>Why associations matter</h2>\n<p>Most real systems have relationships, not isolated records. Modeling associations cleanly helps keep domain logic accurate while still allowing the runtime to translate these relationships into underlying storage constructs.</p>\n",
  },
  {
    slug: "model-inheritance-and-polymorphism",
    folder: "",
    title: "Model Inheritance and Polymorphism",
    summary: "Use inheritance to extend shared model behavior while preserving specialized data contracts for child entities.",
    category: "Model Design",
    readingTime: "4 min read",
    source: "model-inheritance-and-polymorphism.md",
    contentHtml: "<h1>Model Inheritance and Data Polymorphism</h1>\n<p>Object-oriented composition mechanisms map naturally to persistence layers using the declarative <code>inherits</code> configuration parameter. A subclass definition carries all parent columns automatically while adding unique attributes in an extended storage model.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Article&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;id&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">103</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;inherits&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Post&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;source&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Articles&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;fields&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;editorNote&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Text&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;size&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">500</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;wordCount&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Integer&quot;</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><h2>Why inheritance is useful</h2>\n<p>Inheritance keeps shared behavior centralized and makes specialized records easier to maintain. This is especially important in domain models where the base object is common, but child entities need unique attributes or validation logic.</p>\n",
  },
  {
    slug: "data-modelling-with-themost-test",
    folder: "",
    title: "Data Modelling with @themost/test",
    summary: "Use the @themost/test API server as a practical learning environment for modelling entities, attributes, and business domains with @themost-framework.",
    category: "Data Modelling",
    readingTime: "6 min read",
    source: "data-modelling-with-themost-test.md",
    contentHtml: "<h1>Data Modelling with @themost/test</h1>\n<p>A good data model gives an application a stable vocabulary. Instead of scattering table names and storage details throughout the codebase, you describe the business objects your application understands and let the framework expose them consistently.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://github.com/themost-framework/test\"><code>@themost/test</code></a> package is a useful place to explore these ideas. It ships a runnable test API server, a temporary SQLite database, and a small CRM-style domain with customers, products, offers, and orders. You can install it without creating a complete application first:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">npm install @themost/test\nnpx @themost/test\n</code></pre><p>The server exposes the model layer through an OData v4 API. That makes the model visible both in application code and at the HTTP boundary.</p>\n<h2>Start with domain objects</h2>\n<p>The test project defines model classes under <code>modules/test/server/models</code>. A model class represents an entity type and can carry the relationships and behavior that belong to that entity. The <code>Thing</code> model is a useful base for common descriptive fields such as <code>name</code>, <code>description</code>, <code>url</code>, and audit dates:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-js\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> { <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">DataObject</span>, <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">EdmMapping</span> } <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;@themost/data&#x27;</span>\n\n@<span class=\"hljs-title class_\">EdmMapping</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">entityType</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Thing&#x27;</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">class</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">Thing</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">extends</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_ inherited__\">DataObject</span> {\n  <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">constructor</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\"></span>) {\n    <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">super</span>()\n  }\n}\n</code></pre><p>The <code>@EdmMapping.entityType()</code> decorator gives the class a public entity type name. The model can then participate in schema loading, querying, and OData metadata generation.</p>\n<p>This separation is valuable because the class describes the domain while the configured data adapter handles persistence. A caller works with a <code>Thing</code> model rather than having to know which table, connection, or SQL dialect stores it.</p>\n<h2>Reuse meaning through inheritance</h2>\n<p>The test server builds a small inheritance chain:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-text\">Thing\n  └── Account\n        └── User\n</code></pre><p><code>Account</code> extends <code>Thing</code>, and <code>User</code> extends <code>Account</code>. This means shared identity and descriptive attributes can live in one place while specialized models add only what they need. <code>User</code> can add authentication-oriented fields such as <code>enabled</code>, <code>lastLogon</code>, and <code>logonCount</code> without duplicating the base account definition.</p>\n<p>Inheritance is most useful when the relationship is semantic, not merely convenient. A <code>User</code> really is a specialized <code>Account</code>, so the hierarchy communicates something meaningful to the rest of the application. If two objects only happen to share a few columns, a reusable model definition or a relationship may be clearer than inheritance.</p>\n<h2>Let the model drive queries</h2>\n<p>Once an application has a configured <code>DataContext</code>, it can query a model by name. The query expresses what the application wants and remains independent of the underlying provider:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-js\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> users = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">await</span> context.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">model</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;User&#x27;</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">select</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;id&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;name&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;enabled&#x27;</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">where</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;enabled&#x27;</span>).<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">equal</span>(<span class=\"hljs-literal\">true</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">orderBy</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;name&#x27;</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">getItems</span>()\n</code></pre><p>The important detail is that the query starts from the model and its attributes. It does not need to embed a table name or manually assemble a SQL statement. That gives the application a consistent query language as the domain grows and makes provider changes less invasive.</p>\n<h2>A model is a contract</h2>\n<p>A model should answer a few practical questions clearly:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>What does this entity represent?</li>\n<li>Which attributes are required to identify and describe it?</li>\n<li>Which other entities does it relate to?</li>\n<li>Which rules belong to the entity or its lifecycle?</li>\n<li>Which parts should be shared with a base model?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <code>@themost/test</code> project is deliberately approachable because its model classes, configuration, and database can be inspected together. Start with the model definitions, run the server, and compare the code with the generated API metadata. That feedback loop turns modelling from an abstract exercise into something you can query and verify.</p>\n<h2>Next steps</h2>\n<p>Run the test API server and open its schema documentation at <code>/api/docs</code>. The generated model list shows entity types and their inheritance relationships. You can also request the machine-readable metadata document at <code>/api/$metadata</code>.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://github.com/themost-framework/test\">@themost/test repository</a> is a practical companion to the framework: it provides a known domain, real queries, and a working API surface while you learn how models become application behavior.</p>\n",
  },
  {
    slug: "model-inheritance-and-relationships",
    folder: "",
    title: "Model Inheritance and Relationships",
    summary: "See how @themost/test connects entity types with inheritance and relationships, then exposes the model as queryable OData metadata.",
    category: "Data Modelling",
    readingTime: "6 min read",
    source: "model-inheritance-and-relationships.md",
    contentHtml: "<h1>Model Inheritance and Relationships</h1>\n<p>A domain becomes useful when its entities can describe how things fit together. Inheritance captures shared meaning between types. Relationships connect separate entities so an application can navigate the domain without rebuilding joins by hand.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://github.com/themost-framework/test\"><code>@themost/test</code></a> project includes both patterns in a small, inspectable application. Its model classes use <code>@themost/data</code> mappings, while the test API server publishes the resulting model as an OData v4 service.</p>\n<h2>Inheritance describes specialization</h2>\n<p>The sample models use a straightforward hierarchy:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-js\">@<span class=\"hljs-title class_\">EdmMapping</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">entityType</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Account&#x27;</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">class</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">Account</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">extends</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_ inherited__\">Thing</span> {\n  <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">constructor</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\"></span>) {\n    <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">super</span>()\n  }\n}\n\n@<span class=\"hljs-title class_\">EdmMapping</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">entityType</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;User&#x27;</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">class</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">User</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">extends</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_ inherited__\">Account</span> {\n  <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">constructor</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\"></span>) {\n    <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">super</span>()\n  }\n}\n</code></pre><p><code>Account</code> inherits the common properties of <code>Thing</code>. <code>User</code> then specializes <code>Account</code> with user-specific state and behavior, including a <code>getMe()</code> function and credential validation in the test server.</p>\n<p>This gives consumers a coherent contract. A user can be treated as an account when shared behavior is enough, or as a user when the application needs authentication and group membership. The model hierarchy carries that distinction into generated metadata and documentation.</p>\n<h2>Relationships make navigation explicit</h2>\n<p>The sample also models membership relationships. A <code>User</code> exposes a <code>groups</code> collection, and a <code>Group</code> exposes its <code>members</code>. These properties describe the domain directly:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-js\">@<span class=\"hljs-title class_\">EdmMapping</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">entityType</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;User&#x27;</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">class</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">User</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">extends</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_ inherited__\">Account</span> {\n  <span class=\"hljs-comment\">// groups: Group[]</span>\n}\n\n@<span class=\"hljs-title class_\">EdmMapping</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">entityType</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Group&#x27;</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">class</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">Group</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">extends</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_ inherited__\">Account</span> {\n  <span class=\"hljs-comment\">// members: Account[]</span>\n}\n</code></pre><p>The exact storage representation can remain an implementation detail. At the model boundary, callers work with meaningful names such as <code>groups</code> and <code>members</code>. This is easier to understand than carrying foreign-key conventions through every part of an application.</p>\n<p>Relationships also give the query layer enough information to support related data retrieval. For example, an application can request a user and choose whether to include related groups, depending on what the screen or workflow needs. Keeping that choice in the query avoids loading every relationship by default.</p>\n<h2>Inspect the generated contract</h2>\n<p>When <code>@themost/test</code> starts, it creates an <code>ExpressDataApplication</code> and exposes the model layer through <code>/api/</code>. The server provides a metadata document at <code>/api/$metadata</code> and browser-friendly schema documentation at <code>/api/docs</code>.</p>\n<p>You can inspect the metadata with curl:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">curl http://localhost:3000/api/\\<span class=\"hljs-variable\">$metadata</span> \\\n  -H <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Accept: application/xml&#x27;</span>\n</code></pre><p>The document contains entity types and entity sets derived from the configured models. It is a useful modelling check: if an entity, inherited attribute, or relationship is not represented as expected, the model configuration should be corrected before client code is built around it.</p>\n<p>The test server&#39;s documentation route also lists model inheritance. Its model view displays whether a type inherits from or implements another type, which makes the structure easy to verify without reading every source file.</p>\n<h2>Query related data at the API boundary</h2>\n<p>The same model contract is available to API consumers through OData query options. A simple product request from the test project looks like this:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">curl <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;http://localhost:3000/api/Products?%24select=category%2Cprice%2Cmodel%2Cname&#x27;</span> \\\n  -H <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Authorization: Bearer &lt;access-token&gt;&#x27;</span>\n</code></pre><p>The client asks for fields from the <code>Product</code> model, not columns from a particular SQLite table. That distinction is what lets the framework keep domain naming stable while adapters and storage evolve underneath it.</p>\n<h2>Keep relationships purposeful</h2>\n<p>A relationship should describe a real business connection and have a clear direction. Before adding one, ask:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Is this a one-to-one, one-to-many, or many-to-many concept?</li>\n<li>Which side owns the lifecycle or association?</li>\n<li>Should the related collection be loaded for every use case, or only on demand?</li>\n<li>Does the relationship name read naturally in application code?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <code>User</code> and <code>Group</code> example is useful because it represents a real membership concept. It also demonstrates why a model is more than a list of fields: it is a navigable description of the domain.</p>\n<h2>Model first, storage second</h2>\n<p>Inheritance and relationships are most effective when they are designed from the business vocabulary outward. Start with the entities and connections that users recognize, then map those concepts to storage and API behavior. With <code>@themost/test</code>, you can run that model immediately, inspect its metadata, and exercise it through real queries.</p>\n<p>For more examples, explore the <a href=\"https://github.com/themost-framework/test/tree/master/modules/test/server/models\"><code>server/models</code></a> directory and the <a href=\"https://github.com/themost-framework/test/blob/master/README.md\"><code>@themost/test</code> README</a>. They show how a model-driven application can serve both as a production architecture and as a dependable test fixture.</p>\n",
  },
  {
    slug: "order-as-a-multi-relational-model",
    folder: "",
    title: "Order as a Multi-Relational Model",
    summary: "Use the Order schema from @themost/test to understand how one business object can connect customers, offers, products, payments, status, and access rules.",
    category: "Data Modelling",
    readingTime: "7 min read",
    source: "order-as-a-multi-relational-model.md",
    contentHtml: "<h1>Order as a Multi-Relational Model</h1>\n<p>An order looks simple in a user interface, but it represents a network of business facts. Someone placed it, a merchant accepted it, a product was ordered, an offer may have determined its price, payment is due, delivery has a status, and the customer should only see the orders they are allowed to access.</p>\n<p>The <code>Order</code> schema in <a href=\"https://github.com/themost-framework/test/blob/master/modules/test/server/config/models/Order.json\"><code>@themost/test</code></a> is a useful example of this kind of multi-relational model. It shows how <code>@themost-framework</code> can keep the business meaning visible while the database remains an implementation detail.</p>\n<h2>The order is the centre of a graph</h2>\n<p>The schema starts with an identifier and transaction details, then connects the order to other models through typed fields:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Order&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;fields&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;customer&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Person&quot;</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;merchant&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Party&quot;</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;acceptedOffer&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Offer&quot;</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;orderedItem&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Product&quot;</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;orderStatus&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;OrderStatusType&quot;</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;billingAddress&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;PostalAddress&quot;</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;paymentMethod&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;PaymentMethod&quot;</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><p>Each relationship answers a different business question:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>customer</code> identifies the person who placed the order.</li>\n<li><code>merchant</code> identifies the party taking the order.</li>\n<li><code>acceptedOffer</code> records the commercial offer that was accepted.</li>\n<li><code>orderedItem</code> identifies the product being purchased.</li>\n<li><code>orderStatus</code> describes the current point in the order lifecycle.</li>\n<li><code>billingAddress</code> and <code>paymentMethod</code> describe how the transaction is settled.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These are not interchangeable references. Keeping them as separate typed properties prevents an application from collapsing different meanings into a generic <code>relatedId</code> or a collection of opaque foreign keys.</p>\n<h2>Follow relationships to their own models</h2>\n<p>The graph continues beyond the immediate fields. <code>Offer</code> inherits from <code>Intangible</code> and includes its own relationships to a <code>Person</code> seller, an <code>Organization</code> or person offering it, and the <code>Product</code> being offered:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Offer&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;inherits&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Intangible&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;fields&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;price&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Number&quot;</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;validFrom&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;DateTime&quot;</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;validThrough&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;DateTime&quot;</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;seller&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Person&quot;</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;offeredBy&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Organization&quot;</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;itemOffered&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Product&quot;</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><p>That means an order can preserve more than a product reference. It can preserve the accepted commercial context: which offer was selected, its price, when it was valid, who supplied it, and which product it described.</p>\n<p>This distinction matters when prices, promotions, or sellers change later. A query for the current product should not be confused with the offer that was accepted when the order was placed.</p>\n<h2>Model both directions when navigation matters</h2>\n<p>The <code>Person</code> schema contains an <code>orders</code> field with an association mapping back to <code>Order.customer</code>:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;orders&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Order&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;mapping&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;associationType&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;association&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;childModel&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Order&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;childField&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;customer&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;parentModel&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Person&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;parentField&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;id&quot;</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><p>This is the two-sided view of the same relationship. From an order, the application can navigate to its <code>customer</code>. From a person, it can navigate to their <code>orders</code>. The mapping tells the framework how those two model properties correspond without requiring every caller to write the join manually.</p>\n<p>A useful rule is to expose both directions only when the application has a real use for both. Customer history needs <code>Person.orders</code>; checkout and authorization need <code>Order.customer</code>. A relationship should support a workflow, not merely mirror every database link.</p>\n<h2>Keep lifecycle state as a model</h2>\n<p><code>orderStatus</code> is typed as <code>OrderStatusType</code> rather than stored as an unexplained number. The schema provides a default status object with the alternate name <code>OrderProcessing</code>, while the status model can represent other lifecycle states such as delivered or returned.</p>\n<p>That makes status usable at the domain boundary:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-js\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> latestOrders = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">await</span> context.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">model</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Order&#x27;</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">where</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;orderStatus&#x27;</span>).<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">equal</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;OrderProcessing&#x27;</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">orderByDescending</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;orderDate&#x27;</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">getItems</span>()\n</code></pre><p>The important idea is not the exact query syntax. It is that the application can talk about order states by model vocabulary instead of spreading magic values through controllers and views.</p>\n<h2>Turn common relationships into views</h2>\n<p>The <code>Order</code> schema defines two named views:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;views&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;delivered&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;title&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Delivered Orders&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;filter&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;orderStatus eq 1&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;order&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;dateCreated desc&quot;</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;latest&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;title&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Latest Orders&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;filter&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;orderDate gt lastMonth()&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;order&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;dateCreated desc&quot;</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]</span>\n</code></pre><p>A view gives a repeated business query a stable name. The application can ask for the latest orders without duplicating its date filter and sort order in every endpoint. If the definition of “latest” changes, the model configuration is the natural place to update it.</p>\n<p>Views are especially valuable for multi-relational models because they provide a safe starting point for common workflows. A delivered-orders view can later be expanded with related customer or product data without changing the meaning of the view itself.</p>\n<h2>Access rules belong near the model</h2>\n<p>The schema also describes privileges. Global access is granted to configured accounts, while the self-level rule limits a user to orders where <code>customer/user eq me()</code>.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;mask&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;self&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;filter&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;customer/user eq me()&quot;</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><p>This is an important modelling boundary. The <code>Order</code> model knows what it means for an order to belong to a customer, so the access rule can follow the same relationship used by the rest of the application. Authorization does not need to reconstruct ownership through unrelated request parameters.</p>\n<h2>Query the graph deliberately</h2>\n<p>A client can select order fields through the OData API and expand related models when it needs their details. For example, an order summary might request the order number, date, customer, product, and status while leaving payment details out of the response.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-text\">/api/Orders?$select=orderNumber,orderDate,customer,orderedItem,orderStatus\n</code></pre><p>The model makes the graph available; the query decides how much of it to retrieve. That keeps responses focused and avoids loading every relationship for every screen.</p>\n<p>Start the learning server with:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">npx @themost/test\n</code></pre><p>Then inspect the generated schema at <code>/api/docs</code> or <code>/api/$metadata</code>. The documentation reveals the entity types and their attributes, while the metadata endpoint provides the machine-readable contract that clients can use.</p>\n<h2>A model, not a table</h2>\n<p>The <code>Order</code> schema demonstrates the central promise of model-first design. An order is not just a row with a status column. It is a relationship between a customer, merchant, offer, product, address, payment method, and lifecycle state, with reusable views and access rules attached to the same business definition.</p>\n<p>By describing that graph in the model layer, <code>@themost-framework</code> gives application code a durable vocabulary. Storage adapters, API routes, and clients can evolve around the model without each one inventing a different interpretation of what an order means.</p>\n",
  },
  {
    slug: "primitive-types-in-model-schemas",
    folder: "",
    title: "Primitive Types in Model Schemas",
    summary: "Learn how @themost-framework primitive types such as Text, Number, Counter, Boolean, and Date describe the fields in a model.",
    category: "Schema Design",
    readingTime: "4 min read",
    source: "primitive-types-in-model-schemas.md",
    contentHtml: "<h1>Primitive Types in Model Schemas</h1>\n<p>A model schema describes the shape of an entity. Every field has a <code>name</code> and a <code>type</code>, and the type tells <code>@themost-framework</code> what kind of value the field stores and how it should be handled.</p>\n<p>Here is a small model with several common primitive types:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Product&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;fields&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;id&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Counter&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;primary&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-literal\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">true</span></span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Text&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;size&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">120</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;nullable&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-literal\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">false</span></span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;price&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Number&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;nullable&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-literal\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">false</span></span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;available&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Boolean&quot;</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><h2>Text</h2>\n<p><code>Text</code> stores a string value such as a product name, order number, description, or email address.</p>\n<p>Use <code>size</code> when the field has a known maximum length. Use <code>nullable: false</code> when the model requires a value:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;sku&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Text&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;size&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">50</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;nullable&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-literal\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">false</span></span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;unique&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-literal\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">true</span></span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><p>The constraints stay with the model definition, so validation and storage mapping can use the same contract.</p>\n<h2>Number</h2>\n<p><code>Number</code> stores numeric values such as prices, quantities, ratings, or measurements:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;quantity&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Number&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;nullable&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-literal\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">false</span></span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><p>Use a numeric type for values that will be calculated or compared. A price should not be stored as <code>Text</code>, because sorting the strings <code>&quot;9.50&quot;</code> and <code>&quot;100.00&quot;</code> would not have the same meaning as sorting numeric values.</p>\n<p>For values that represent money, the schema can use the more specific <code>Currency</code> type. The type communicates the intention of the field to both the framework and the people reading the model.</p>\n<h2>Counter</h2>\n<p><code>Counter</code> is commonly used for an automatically generated identifier:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;id&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Counter&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;primary&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-literal\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">true</span></span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><p>A counter is useful when the storage provider should assign a new numeric key as records are created. Application code can create a new entity without inventing an identifier first.</p>\n<p>A primary counter identifies the record. It is different from a business number such as an order number, which may have its own formatting, visibility, and generation rules.</p>\n<h2>Integer</h2>\n<p><code>Integer</code> describes a whole number without a fractional part. It is useful for counts, flags represented as numbers, or references that are intentionally numeric:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;sortOrder&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Integer&quot;</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><p>Choose <code>Integer</code> when fractions do not make sense. Choose <code>Number</code> when a value may contain a decimal part or when the domain calls for general numeric behavior.</p>\n<h2>Boolean</h2>\n<p><code>Boolean</code> stores a true-or-false value:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;enabled&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Boolean&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;nullable&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-literal\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">false</span></span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><p>Boolean fields are a good fit for switches such as <code>enabled</code>, <code>isGift</code>, or <code>isArchived</code>. If a field must always have a decision, make it non-nullable so the model does not have to interpret a missing value.</p>\n<h2>Date and DateTime</h2>\n<p>Use <code>Date</code> or <code>DateTime</code> for temporal values. Typical examples include creation dates, order dates, due dates, and the moment an entity was last modified:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;orderDate&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;DateTime&quot;</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><p>A date-only field represents a calendar date. A date-time field represents a point in time and is appropriate when time and ordering matter. Keeping temporal values typed also lets queries compare them as dates instead of treating them as arbitrary strings.</p>\n<h2>Primitive fields and model relationships</h2>\n<p>Not every field points to a primitive value. A field whose type is another model describes a relationship:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;customer&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Person&quot;</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><p><code>customer</code> is not a string containing a person name. It connects <code>Order</code> to the <code>Person</code> model. The distinction matters because related models can have their own fields, rules, and relationships.</p>\n<p>A quick way to recognize the difference is:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>Text</code>, <code>Number</code>, <code>Integer</code>, <code>Boolean</code>, <code>Date</code>, and <code>DateTime</code> describe values.</li>\n<li><code>Counter</code> commonly describes a generated identity value.</li>\n<li><code>Currency</code> describes a monetary value.</li>\n<li>A type such as <code>Person</code>, <code>Product</code>, or <code>OrderStatusType</code> describes a related model or domain type.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Start with the domain value</h2>\n<p>When adding a field, choose the type that best describes the business meaning first. Then add constraints such as <code>nullable</code>, <code>size</code>, <code>unique</code>, <code>primary</code>, or a calculated <code>value</code> where the model needs them.</p>\n<p>A clear schema gives the framework a shared contract for storage, validation, querying, and API metadata. Small type choices such as <code>Text</code> versus <code>Number</code> become valuable because they keep the model honest as the application grows.</p>\n",
  },
  {
    slug: "data-privileges",
    folder: "",
    title: "Data Privileges",
    summary: "Learn the basics of model-level access rules in @themost/data using global, account, and self privileges.",
    category: "Security",
    readingTime: "4 min read",
    source: "data-privileges.md",
    contentHtml: "<h1>Data Privileges</h1>\n<p>A model should describe not only what data looks like, but also who can access it. In <code>@themost/data</code>, privileges are declared in the model schema and define which users or accounts can perform operations on an entity.</p>\n<p>A simple privilege definition looks like this:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;privileges&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;mask&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">15</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;global&quot;</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><p>Keeping access rules near the model makes them easier to understand and reuse. API routes, background jobs, and other callers can use the same model contract instead of each implementing a different permission check.</p>\n<h2>The privilege mask</h2>\n<p>The <code>mask</code> value describes the operations granted by the privilege. It is represented as a number so several operation permissions can be combined in one rule.</p>\n<p>The exact mask values depend on the framework&#39;s permission definitions, but the important starting point is simple: a larger combined mask can grant several operations, while a smaller mask can grant a narrower capability. Existing schemas commonly use <code>15</code> for broad administrative access and <code>1</code> for a restricted rule.</p>\n<p>Treat the mask as part of the schema contract. Prefer the framework&#39;s named permission constants or the conventions used by your application when defining new rules, rather than scattering unexplained numbers through application code.</p>\n<h2>Global access</h2>\n<p>A <code>global</code> privilege applies across the model without limiting access to a particular account or record:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;mask&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">15</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;global&quot;</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><p>This is useful for system-wide defaults. For example, a model might allow general access to authenticated users while using a more specific account or self rule for sensitive operations.</p>\n<p>Global does not necessarily mean anonymous. Authentication and the model&#39;s privilege rules work together. The application still decides whether a request has an authenticated user and then evaluates which privileges that user can use.</p>\n<h2>Account-specific access</h2>\n<p>A privilege can target an account by including the <code>account</code> property:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;mask&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">15</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;global&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;account&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Administrators&quot;</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><p>This grants the specified operations to members of the <code>Administrators</code> account. Account-based rules are useful for roles such as administrators, editors, support agents, or managers.</p>\n<p>The account name is part of your application&#39;s authorization vocabulary. Keep those names consistent with the accounts configured in the application, and avoid relying on a role name that exists only in one controller or route.</p>\n<h2>Self-level access</h2>\n<p>A <code>self</code> privilege limits access to records related to the current user:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;mask&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;self&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;filter&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;customer/user eq me()&quot;</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><p>This pattern appears in the <code>Order</code> schema from <a href=\"https://github.com/themost-framework/test/blob/master/modules/test/server/config/models/Order.json\"><code>@themost/test</code></a>. It says that the current user can access orders whose customer is connected to that user.</p>\n<p>The <code>filter</code> is evaluated using the model&#39;s fields and relationships. Because it follows the <code>customer</code> relationship, the rule remains aligned with the domain model instead of trusting an arbitrary customer identifier supplied by the request.</p>\n<h2>Combine rules carefully</h2>\n<p>A model can define multiple privileges for different audiences:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;privileges&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;mask&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">15</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;global&quot;</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;mask&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">15</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;global&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;account&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Administrators&quot;</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;mask&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;self&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;filter&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;customer/user eq me()&quot;</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><p>Read this as a set of access rules, not as application prose. Each entry has a target, a scope, and an operation mask. When a rule becomes difficult to explain, split it into smaller rules or give the model a clearer relationship to follow.</p>\n<h2>Privileges and relationships</h2>\n<p>Privileges become especially useful on relational models. An <code>Order</code> is connected to a <code>Person</code> through <code>customer</code>, so the order&#39;s self rule can follow that association. Similar rules can protect documents owned by a user, tasks assigned to a user, or messages addressed to a user.</p>\n<p>The relationship must be part of the model contract. If a filter depends on a field that is missing, ambiguously named, or mapped differently across models, the access rule will be difficult to maintain and verify.</p>\n<h2>Start with least privilege</h2>\n<p>When designing a new model, begin with the smallest access required by each user group. Then add broader account privileges only where the workflow needs them. Test both allowed and denied cases, including a user who owns a record, a user who does not own it, and an administrator.</p>\n<p>Privileges are one part of the security design. They should be combined with authentication, input validation, and careful response shaping. Their strength is that the model can express the data ownership rules once, close to the relationships and fields that define the domain.</p>\n<p>For a working example, start the <code>@themost/test</code> server and inspect the model documentation at <code>/api/docs</code>. The <code>Order</code> schema shows how privileges, relationships, views, and business fields can live together in one readable definition.</p>\n",
  },
  {
    slug: "model-event-listeners",
    folder: "",
    title: "Model Event Listeners",
    summary: "Learn how @themost/data model eventListeners run during save, remove, query, and upgrade operations.",
    category: "Event Model",
    readingTime: "5 min read",
    source: "model-event-listeners.md",
    contentHtml: "<h1>Model Event Listeners</h1>\n<p>A model event listener is a small module that runs when something happens to a data model. It can validate an object before saving, react after a new record is created, prevent a delete, or observe a query before it reaches the data provider.</p>\n<p>Listeners are useful when a rule belongs to the model and should run no matter which part of the application performs the operation. A web controller, a background job, and a test can all use the same model lifecycle.</p>\n<h2>Register a listener in the schema</h2>\n<p>A model declares its listeners with the <code>eventListeners</code> property. Each entry points to a module and may include a readable name:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Order&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;eventListeners&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;New Order Notification&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;/app/controllers/new-order-listener&quot;</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><p>When the model is loaded, <code>@themost/data</code> resolves the module and registers any supported listener methods it exports. A listener can also refer to a particular exported class or object with a <code>#</code> suffix, such as <code>./listeners/OnUpdateProduct#OnUpdateProduct</code>.</p>\n<h2>Write a before-save listener</h2>\n<p>A listener method receives two arguments: an event object and a callback. The event contains information about the operation, including the target object and the model. The callback tells the framework that the listener has finished:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">export</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">function</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">beforeSave</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">event, callback</span>) {\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> order = event.<span class=\"hljs-property\">target</span>;\n\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">if</span> (!order.<span class=\"hljs-property\">orderNumber</span>) {\n    order.<span class=\"hljs-property\">orderNumber</span> = <span class=\"hljs-string\">`ORDER-<span class=\"hljs-subst\">${<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">Date</span>.now()}</span>`</span>;\n  }\n\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">callback</span>();\n}\n</code></pre><p>A <code>beforeSave</code> listener runs before the object is persisted. It can normalize values, calculate a field, or reject invalid state. To stop the operation, pass an error to the callback:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">export</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">function</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">beforeSave</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">event, callback</span>) {\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">if</span> (event.<span class=\"hljs-property\">target</span>.<span class=\"hljs-property\">total</span> &lt; <span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>) {\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">callback</span>(<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">Error</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Order total cannot be negative.&#x27;</span>));\n  }\n\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">callback</span>();\n}\n</code></pre><p>The callback is important. If the listener does not call it, the save pipeline cannot continue or finish correctly.</p>\n<h2>React after a save</h2>\n<p>An <code>afterSave</code> listener runs after an object has been created or updated. It is a good place for side effects such as sending a notification, writing an audit entry, or updating an external system:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">export</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">function</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">afterSave</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">event, callback</span>) {\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">if</span> (event.<span class=\"hljs-property\">state</span> !== <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>) {\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">callback</span>();\n  }\n\n  <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">console</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">log</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;A new order was created:&#x27;</span>, event.<span class=\"hljs-property\">target</span>.<span class=\"hljs-property\">orderNumber</span>);\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">callback</span>();\n}\n</code></pre><p>The <code>state</code> value tells the listener what kind of save occurred. Check it before performing an action that should happen only for a new record. A notification that belongs only to creation should not be sent again every time the order is edited.</p>\n<p>External work should be handled carefully. If a notification is essential to the business operation, decide whether an error should fail the save. If it is optional, log the failure and keep the persistence workflow reliable according to the application&#39;s requirements.</p>\n<h2>Other lifecycle methods</h2>\n<p>A listener module can implement more than one lifecycle method:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">export</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">function</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">beforeRemove</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">event, callback</span>) {\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">if</span> (event.<span class=\"hljs-property\">target</span>.<span class=\"hljs-property\">isProtected</span>) {\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">callback</span>(<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">Error</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;This record cannot be removed.&#x27;</span>));\n  }\n\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">callback</span>();\n}\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">export</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">function</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">afterRemove</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">event, callback</span>) {\n  <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">console</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">log</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Removed:&#x27;</span>, event.<span class=\"hljs-property\">target</span>.<span class=\"hljs-property\">id</span>);\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">callback</span>();\n}\n</code></pre><p>Common model events include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>beforeSave</code>: validate or prepare an object before insert or update.</li>\n<li><code>afterSave</code>: react after insert or update completes.</li>\n<li><code>beforeRemove</code>: prevent or prepare a delete.</li>\n<li><code>afterRemove</code>: react after deletion completes.</li>\n<li><code>beforeExecute</code>: inspect or change a query before execution.</li>\n<li><code>afterExecute</code>: inspect the result after a query executes.</li>\n<li><code>beforeUpgrade</code> and <code>afterUpgrade</code>: respond when a model is upgraded or migrated.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The framework only registers methods that the listener exports. A module can therefore focus on the events it actually needs.</p>\n<h2>Understand the event object</h2>\n<p>The event argument is a <code>DataEventArgs</code> object. Its most useful properties are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>model</code>: the <code>DataModel</code> that raised the event.</li>\n<li><code>target</code>: the data object being inserted, updated, or removed.</li>\n<li><code>state</code>: the operation state, such as insert, update, or delete.</li>\n<li><code>previous</code>: the previous version of the object when it is available.</li>\n<li><code>emitter</code>: the query or event emitter involved in the operation.</li>\n<li><code>query</code>: the underlying query expression when the event concerns execution.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For example, an update listener can compare the current and previous values:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">export</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">function</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">beforeSave</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">event, callback</span>) {\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">if</span> (event.<span class=\"hljs-property\">state</span> === <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span> &amp;&amp; event.<span class=\"hljs-property\">previous</span>) {\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">if</span> (event.<span class=\"hljs-property\">previous</span>.<span class=\"hljs-property\">status</span> !== event.<span class=\"hljs-property\">target</span>.<span class=\"hljs-property\">status</span>) {\n      <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">console</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">log</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">`Status changed on <span class=\"hljs-subst\">${event.model.name}</span>`</span>);\n    }\n  }\n\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">callback</span>();\n}\n</code></pre><p>Use the event information instead of reading request-specific variables from inside the listener. That keeps the listener reusable wherever the model is used.</p>\n<h2>Keep listeners focused</h2>\n<p>A listener should enforce one model rule or coordinate one small piece of lifecycle behavior. Good listeners are predictable, finish quickly, and always call their callback.</p>\n<p>Avoid putting an entire application workflow inside a single listener. If several operations are involved, call a dedicated service from the listener and keep the model event method responsible for translating lifecycle events into that service call.</p>\n<p>Also remember that listeners may run for every operation on the model. A slow network request in <code>beforeSave</code>, for example, can slow every save. Choose <code>before</code> or <code>after</code> based on whether the operation must be blocked by the listener&#39;s work.</p>\n<h2>The listener lifecycle</h2>\n<p>The basic flow looks like this:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-text\">application calls model.save(object)\n        |\n        v\nbeforeSave listeners run\n        |\n        v\nobject is persisted, or an error stops the operation\n        |\n        v\nafterSave listeners run\n</code></pre><p>This is the main idea behind model event listeners: the model owns the lifecycle, while each listener contributes one focused rule or reaction. Once the listener calls its callback, the framework can move to the next listener or finish the operation.</p>\n<p>Start with a <code>beforeSave</code> validation rule, test the allowed and rejected cases, and then add an <code>afterSave</code> reaction when the persisted change needs to notify another part of the system.</p>\n",
  }
]

export const articleBySlug = {
  "themost-framework-application-structure": {
    slug: "themost-framework-application-structure",
    folder: "",
    title: "The @themost-framework Application Structure",
    summary: "Use @themost/test as a map for organizing a @themost-framework application, from configuration and models to routes, authentication, and the database.",
    category: "Application Architecture",
    readingTime: "7 min read",
    source: "themost-framework-application-structure.md",
    contentHtml: "<h1>The @themost-framework Application Structure</h1>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://github.com/themost-framework/test\"><code>@themost/test</code></a> package is more than a test database. It is a small, working <code>@themost-framework</code> application that shows how configuration, models, Express routes, authentication, and storage fit together.</p>\n<p>Its server directory follows a useful separation of responsibilities:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-text\">modules/test/server/\n├── app.js\n├── standalone.js\n├── config/\n│   ├── app.json\n│   ├── models/\n│   └── public.pem / private.key\n├── db/\n│   └── local.db\n├── models/\n├── routes/\n└── views/\n</code></pre><p>The exact contents can grow with an application, but the boundary between these areas is the important part.</p>\n<h2><code>app.js</code> is the composition root</h2>\n<p>The application starts in <code>server/app.js</code>. Its <code>getApplication()</code> function creates the Express instance and composes the framework services:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> app = <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">express</span>()\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> serviceApplication = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">ExpressDataApplication</span>(\n  path.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">resolve</span>(applicationDir, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;config&#x27;</span>),\n)\n\napp.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">use</span>(express.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">json</span>({ <span class=\"hljs-attr\">reviver</span>: dateReviver }))\napp.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">use</span>(serviceApplication.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">middleware</span>(app))\n</code></pre><p>This file is the composition root. It wires together the configuration directory, the data application, authentication, routes, metadata, error handling, and views. It should assemble the application rather than become the place where every model rule is implemented.</p>\n<p>The data middleware is especially important. It creates an <code>ExpressDataContext</code> for a request and exposes it as <code>req.context</code>, allowing routes and services to query models through the active configuration.</p>\n<h2>Configuration is the application contract</h2>\n<p>The <code>server/config</code> directory is where the application declares how it should operate. Its central <code>app.json</code> configuration connects model loading, database adapters, and runtime settings.</p>\n<p>A simplified configuration shape looks like this:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;adapterTypes&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Test Data Adapter&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;invariantName&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;sqlite&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;@themost/sqlite#SqliteAdapter&quot;</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;adapters&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;test&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;invariantName&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;test&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;default&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-literal\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">true</span></span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;options&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n        <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;database&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;./db/local.db&quot;</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;settings&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;api&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;origin&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;http://localhost:3000&quot;</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><p>The exact values can differ by version and environment, but the responsibilities remain recognizable:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>adapterTypes</code> maps an invariant name to an adapter implementation.</li>\n<li><code>adapters</code> defines named connections and their options.</li>\n<li><code>default</code> selects the connection used when no other adapter is requested.</li>\n<li><code>settings</code> contains application-level options such as API origin and authentication behavior.</li>\n<li>Model definitions are loaded from the configured schema location, normally <code>config/models</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This arrangement keeps deployment choices out of model files. A model can say that <code>Order</code> has a <code>customer</code> and an <code>orderStatus</code>; the configuration decides which database stores those entities.</p>\n<h2>Model schemas define the domain</h2>\n<p>The <code>config/models</code> directory contains JSON schema definitions such as <code>Order.json</code>, <code>Person.json</code>, <code>Product.json</code>, and <code>User.json</code>. These files describe the domain vocabulary:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Product&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;fields&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;id&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Counter&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;primary&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-literal\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">true</span></span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Text&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;nullable&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-literal\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">false</span></span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;price&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Number&quot;</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><p>Schemas can also define relationships, inheritance, views, privileges, constraints, and event listeners. This is where the model contract belongs. It is not an Express route and it is not a database migration script.</p>\n<p>The framework&#39;s schema loader reads these definitions and makes them available through <code>context.model(&#39;Product&#39;)</code>. The OData model builder can then expose the same definitions as entity types, entity sets, and metadata.</p>\n<h2><code>models</code> adds executable behavior</h2>\n<p>The <code>server/models</code> directory contains JavaScript model classes for behavior that is easier to express in code. In <code>@themost/test</code>, the model classes build a hierarchy such as:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-text\">Thing -&gt; Account -&gt; User\n</code></pre><p>A class can add functions, validation helpers, or specialized behavior while the JSON schema continues to describe fields and relationships:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\">@<span class=\"hljs-title class_\">EdmMapping</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">entityType</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;User&#x27;</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">class</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">User</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">extends</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_ inherited__\">Account</span> {\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">static</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">async</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">getMe</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">context</span>) {\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> context.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">model</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;User&#x27;</span>)\n      .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">where</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;name&#x27;</span>).<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">equal</span>(context.<span class=\"hljs-property\">user</span> &amp;&amp; context.<span class=\"hljs-property\">user</span>.<span class=\"hljs-property\">name</span>)\n      .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">getTypedItem</span>()\n  }\n}\n</code></pre><p>This gives the application two complementary modelling tools. JSON schemas are excellent for declarative metadata. Model classes are useful when a domain operation needs executable logic.</p>\n<h2><code>db</code> holds the test storage</h2>\n<p>The <code>server/db</code> directory contains the SQLite fixture used by the test application. The repository describes it as a CRM-style database with customers, products, offers, orders, and related objects.</p>\n<p>When <code>@themost/test</code> starts its built-in application, it copies the local database to a temporary location before using it. That keeps test runs isolated and prevents one test run from permanently changing the repository fixture.</p>\n<p>This is a good pattern for a learning environment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The schema is inspectable.</li>\n<li>The database contains realistic relationships.</li>\n<li>Each run can start from known data.</li>\n<li>The same application can be used by tests and API clients.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In a production application, the adapter options would normally point to a managed database or an environment-specific database location instead of a copied local fixture.</p>\n<h2><code>routes</code> owns HTTP behavior</h2>\n<p>The <code>server/routes</code> directory contains Express routers for concerns such as authentication, documentation, and the application home page. These routes use <code>req.context</code> when they need model data, but they do not need to know how a database connection was created.</p>\n<p>The test application also mounts the framework&#39;s <code>serviceRouter</code> under <code>/api/</code>. That router exposes model-driven operations and metadata through the OData service surface. Custom routes can still be added for workflows that need a response or process beyond ordinary entity operations.</p>\n<p>A useful route remains small:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\">router.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">get</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;/me&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">async</span> (req, res, next) =&gt; {\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">try</span> {\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> user = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">await</span> req.<span class=\"hljs-property\">context</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">model</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;User&#x27;</span>)\n      .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">where</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;name&#x27;</span>).<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">equal</span>(req.<span class=\"hljs-property\">context</span>.<span class=\"hljs-property\">user</span>.<span class=\"hljs-property\">name</span>)\n      .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">getItem</span>()\n\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> res.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">json</span>(user)\n  } <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">catch</span> (error) {\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">next</span>(error)\n  }\n})\n</code></pre><p>The route handles HTTP input and output. The model handles the data vocabulary and query behavior.</p>\n<h2>Authentication is an application service</h2>\n<p>The test server registers an <code>Authenticator</code> application service. Its configuration reads JWT key paths from the application settings, and its routes expose token and login behavior under <code>/auth/</code>.</p>\n<p>The authentication flow is separate from the data model, but it uses the model context to validate users and clients. Once Passport authenticates a request, <code>@themost/express</code> makes the authenticated user available through the request context.</p>\n<p>This matters for model privileges. An <code>Order</code> privilege such as <code>customer/user eq me()</code> can evaluate ownership using the same authenticated identity that the route and service layer use.</p>\n<p>Keep private keys and credentials outside source-controlled configuration. The test project includes key files because it is a self-contained learning and testing environment; a deployed application should load secrets securely.</p>\n<h2><code>standalone.js</code> is the launcher</h2>\n<p>The <code>server/standalone.js</code> file is a command-line entry point. It creates the application with <code>getApplication()</code>, applies options such as port, host, and API origin, and starts it with <code>serveApplication()</code>:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> app = <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">getApplication</span>(cwd)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> service = app.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">get</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;ExpressDataApplication&#x27;</span>)\nservice.<span class=\"hljs-property\">configuration</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">setSourceAt</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;settings/api/origin&#x27;</span>, origin)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">await</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">serveApplication</span>(app, port, host)\n</code></pre><p>Keeping the launcher separate from <code>app.js</code> makes the application easy to reuse. Tests can import <code>getApplication()</code> without starting a permanent process, while the command-line package can choose its own port and host.</p>\n<h2>A request through the structure</h2>\n<p>A request to the test API passes through the layers in a predictable order:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-text\">client request\n    |\n    v\nExpress routes and authentication\n    |\n    v\nExpressDataApplication middleware\n    |\n    v\nrequest-scoped ExpressDataContext\n    |\n    v\nmodel schema and model behavior\n    |\n    v\n@themost/query expression\n    |\n    v\nconfigured database adapter\n    |\n    v\nSQLite, PostgreSQL, MySQL, Oracle, or another provider\n</code></pre><p>The same model definition can therefore participate in HTTP APIs, background jobs, tests, privileges, listeners, and database operations without each layer inventing a separate representation.</p>\n<h2>A useful starting structure</h2>\n<p>When creating a new application, begin with the shape demonstrated by <code>@themost/test</code>:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-text\">server/\n├── app.js              # compose the application\n├── config/\n│   ├── app.json        # adapters and runtime settings\n│   └── models/         # domain schemas\n├── models/             # executable model behavior\n├── routes/             # HTTP-specific routes and services\n├── db/                 # local or test database fixtures\n└── views/              # server-rendered pages, if needed\n</code></pre><p>The most important habit is to keep configuration explicit. Put adapter selection, model loading, API origin, authentication settings, and environment-specific options in configuration. Put domain shape in schemas. Put executable domain behavior in model classes or listeners. Put HTTP behavior in routes and services.</p>\n<p>That separation is what makes <code>@themost/test</code> useful as a learning application: every concern has a visible home, but all of them meet through the same <code>ExpressDataApplication</code> and model context.</p>\n",
  },
  "themost-express": {
    slug: "themost-express",
    folder: "",
    title: "Getting Started with @themost/express",
    summary: "Connect @themost/data to an Express application with request contexts, model queries, and the built-in service router.",
    category: "Express Integration",
    readingTime: "5 min read",
    source: "themost-express.md",
    contentHtml: "<h1>Getting Started with @themost/express</h1>\n<p><code>@themost/express</code> connects the model-first data layer of <code>@themost/data</code> to an Express.js application. It gives each request a data context, makes your configured models available through <code>req.context</code>, and provides a router for exposing those models as an API.</p>\n<p>The result is a useful boundary between HTTP and the domain model:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-text\">HTTP request -&gt; ExpressDataApplication -&gt; DataContext -&gt; Model query\n</code></pre><p>Install the package alongside Express:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">npm install express @themost/express\n</code></pre><h2>Create a data application</h2>\n<p>The central class is <code>ExpressDataApplication</code>. It loads the application configuration and initializes the model builder and service infrastructure:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> express <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;express&#x27;</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> path <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;node:path&#x27;</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> { <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">ExpressDataApplication</span>, dateReviver } <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;@themost/express&#x27;</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> app = <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">express</span>()\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> dataApplication = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">ExpressDataApplication</span>(\n  path.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">resolve</span>(process.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">cwd</span>(), <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;config&#x27;</span>),\n)\n\napp.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">use</span>(express.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">json</span>({ <span class=\"hljs-attr\">reviver</span>: dateReviver }))\napp.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">use</span>(dataApplication.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">middleware</span>(app))\n</code></pre><p>The configuration path points to the directory containing the application configuration, adapters, and model schemas. You can also construct the application with a configuration object when that is more convenient for tests or small applications.</p>\n<h2>Request-scoped data contexts</h2>\n<p>After the middleware is registered, each request receives a <code>context</code> property:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\">app.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">get</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;/people&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">async</span> (req, res, next) =&gt; {\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">try</span> {\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> people = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">await</span> req.<span class=\"hljs-property\">context</span>\n      .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">model</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Person&#x27;</span>)\n      .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">select</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;id&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;name&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;email&#x27;</span>)\n      .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">orderBy</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;name&#x27;</span>)\n      .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">getItems</span>()\n\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> res.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">json</span>(people)\n  } <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">catch</span> (error) {\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">next</span>(error)\n  }\n})\n</code></pre><p><code>req.context</code> is an <code>ExpressDataContext</code>. It is connected to the current application configuration and is the normal entry point for model queries during a request. The middleware also finalizes the context when the response closes, helping release request-specific data resources consistently.</p>\n<p>This is the important difference from putting a shared database object directly on the Express application: the context belongs to the request, while the application holds the reusable configuration and services.</p>\n<h2>Use the built-in service router</h2>\n<p>When you want to expose your configured entity models through the framework&#39;s standard API, use <code>serviceRouter</code>:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> passport <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;passport&#x27;</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> { serviceRouter } <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;@themost/express&#x27;</span>\n\napp.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">use</span>(\n  <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;/api&#x27;</span>,\n  passport.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">authenticate</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;bearer&#x27;</span>, { <span class=\"hljs-attr\">session</span>: <span class=\"hljs-literal\">false</span> }),\n  serviceRouter,\n)\n</code></pre><p>The router provides common operations for entity sets, individual entities, navigation properties, functions, actions, and metadata. The exact access rules still come from your model configuration and authentication setup.</p>\n<p>A client can then make a request such as:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-text\">GET /api/People?$select=id,name,email\n</code></pre><p>The API is model-driven: <code>People</code> is resolved from the configured model definitions, and the query options are translated into the data layer rather than being manually converted into SQL inside a route.</p>\n<h2>Traditional Express routes still work</h2>\n<p>You do not have to use the service router for every endpoint. A regular route can use the same request context and add application-specific behavior:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\">app.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">post</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;/people&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">async</span> (req, res, next) =&gt; {\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">try</span> {\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> person = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">await</span> req.<span class=\"hljs-property\">context</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">model</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Person&#x27;</span>).<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">save</span>(req.<span class=\"hljs-property\">body</span>)\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> res.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">status</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">201</span>).<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">json</span>(person)\n  } <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">catch</span> (error) {\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">next</span>(error)\n  }\n})\n</code></pre><p>This gives you a practical choice. Use the service router for consistent model APIs, and use ordinary Express routes when an endpoint needs a custom workflow, response, or integration with another service.</p>\n<h2>Run work outside a request</h2>\n<p>Background jobs and command-line tasks do not have <code>req.context</code>. <code>ExpressDataApplication</code> can create a context directly, or execute a callback and finalize the context for you:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\">dataApplication.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">execute</span>(<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">async</span> (context, done) =&gt; {\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">try</span> {\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> latestPeople = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">await</span> context\n      .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">model</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Person&#x27;</span>)\n      .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">where</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;dateModified&#x27;</span>).<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">greaterThan</span>(<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">Date</span>(<span class=\"hljs-title class_\">Date</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">now</span>() - <span class=\"hljs-number\">86400000</span>))\n      .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">getItems</span>()\n\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">done</span>(<span class=\"hljs-literal\">null</span>, latestPeople)\n  } <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">catch</span> (error) {\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">done</span>(error)\n  }\n}, <span class=\"hljs-function\">(<span class=\"hljs-params\">error, value</span>) =&gt;</span> {\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">if</span> (error) {\n    <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">console</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">error</span>(error)\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span>\n  }\n\n  <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">console</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">log</span>(value)\n})\n</code></pre><p>For promise-based code, create a context directly and finalize it when the operation completes. The key rule is the same as with any data context: finish the context after the work is done.</p>\n<h2>Extend the application</h2>\n<p><code>ExpressDataApplication</code> supports application services and strategies. A service can add routes or coordinate a cross-cutting capability while still receiving the configured application instance:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">class</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">HealthService</span> {\n  <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">constructor</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">application</span>) {\n    application.<span class=\"hljs-property\">container</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">subscribe</span>(<span class=\"hljs-function\">(<span class=\"hljs-params\">container</span>) =&gt;</span> {\n      <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">if</span> (container) {\n        container.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">get</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;/health&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-function\">(<span class=\"hljs-params\">req, res</span>) =&gt;</span> {\n          res.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">json</span>({ <span class=\"hljs-attr\">status</span>: <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;ok&#x27;</span> })\n        })\n      }\n    })\n  }\n}\n\ndataApplication.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">useService</span>(<span class=\"hljs-title class_\">HealthService</span>)\n</code></pre><p>This keeps extension points inside the application container instead of coupling every feature to the top-level Express setup.</p>\n<h2>The Express boundary</h2>\n<p><code>@themost/express</code> is deliberately focused. Express remains responsible for HTTP concerns such as routing, authentication middleware, and response handling. <code>@themost/data</code> remains responsible for models, queries, validation, relationships, privileges, and persistence. <code>@themost/express</code> gives them a request-aware context and a standard API path between them.</p>\n<p>Start with <code>ExpressDataApplication</code>, register its middleware before routes that need data access, and use <code>req.context.model(&#39;ModelName&#39;)</code> inside those routes. From there, the same model definitions can serve custom Express endpoints, the built-in service router, background work, and tests.</p>\n<p>For the complete integration surface, explore the <a href=\"https://github.com/themost-framework/express\"><code>@themost/express</code> repository</a> and its <a href=\"https://github.com/themost-framework/express#usage\">usage examples</a>.</p>\n",
  },
  "database-agnostic-query": {
    slug: "database-agnostic-query",
    folder: "",
    title: "A Database-Agnostic Query with @themost/query",
    summary: "Build readable queries with JavaScript expressions and let @themost/query translate them for different database engines.",
    category: "Querying",
    readingTime: "5 min read",
    source: "database-agnostic-query.md",
    contentHtml: "<h1>A Database-Agnostic Query with @themost/query</h1>\n<p><code>@themost/query</code> is the database-agnostic query module behind <code>@themost/data</code>. It lets application code describe filtering, selection, sorting, joins, and paging without writing a database-specific SQL string for every operation.</p>\n<p>The package separates two jobs:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Build a query expression that describes what data is needed.</li>\n<li>Let a formatter or data adapter translate and execute that expression for the configured database.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>That separation is what makes the same query useful with SQLite, PostgreSQL, MySQL, SQL Server, Oracle, and other supported adapters.</p>\n<h2>Install the query module</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">npm install @themost/query\n</code></pre><p>Most application code reaches <code>@themost/query</code> through <code>@themost/data</code>, but the query package can also be used directly when building adapter-level tools or reusable query definitions.</p>\n<h2>Build a simple query</h2>\n<p>A <code>QueryExpression</code> can select fields, identify its source entity, sort the result, and limit the number of rows:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> { <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">QueryExpression</span>, <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">SqlFormatter</span> } <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;@themost/query&#x27;</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> query = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">QueryExpression</span>()\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">select</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;id&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;name&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;category&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;price&#x27;</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">from</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;ProductData&#x27;</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">orderBy</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;name&#x27;</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">take</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">25</span>)\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> sql = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">SqlFormatter</span>().<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">format</span>(query)\n</code></pre><p>The expression describes the operation. <code>SqlFormatter</code> turns it into SQL for inspection or for use by a compatible adapter. The application does not need to concatenate column names and values into a SQL string by hand.</p>\n<h2>Filter with a field expression</h2>\n<p>The fluent API supports comparison methods such as <code>equal</code>, <code>greaterThan</code>, <code>lowerThan</code>, <code>in</code>, and their related operators:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> query = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">QueryExpression</span>()\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">select</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;id&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;name&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;price&#x27;</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">from</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;ProductData&#x27;</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">where</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;category&#x27;</span>).<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">equal</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Laptops&#x27;</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">and</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;price&#x27;</span>).<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">greaterThan</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">500</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">orderByDescending</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;price&#x27;</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">take</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>)\n</code></pre><p>The query expresses a business condition: find laptop products that cost more than 500 and return the most expensive ten first. The adapter is responsible for producing the syntax required by its database engine.</p>\n<h2>Use JavaScript closures</h2>\n<p>One of the distinctive features of <code>@themost/query</code> is its closure-to-query support. Instead of writing every condition through string methods, you can describe it with a JavaScript expression:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> { <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">QueryEntity</span>, <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">QueryExpression</span>, <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">SqlFormatter</span> } <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;@themost/query&#x27;</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> products = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">QueryEntity</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;ProductData&#x27;</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> query = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">QueryExpression</span>()\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">select</span>(<span class=\"hljs-function\">(<span class=\"hljs-params\">product</span>) =&gt;</span> ({\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">id</span>: product.<span class=\"hljs-property\">id</span>,\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">name</span>: product.<span class=\"hljs-property\">name</span>,\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">price</span>: product.<span class=\"hljs-property\">price</span>,\n  }))\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">from</span>(products)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">where</span>(<span class=\"hljs-function\">(<span class=\"hljs-params\">product</span>) =&gt;</span> product.<span class=\"hljs-property\">price</span> &gt; <span class=\"hljs-number\">500</span> &amp;&amp; product.<span class=\"hljs-property\">price</span> &lt;= <span class=\"hljs-number\">1000</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">orderBy</span>(<span class=\"hljs-function\">(<span class=\"hljs-params\">product</span>) =&gt;</span> product.<span class=\"hljs-property\">price</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">take</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>)\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> sql = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">SqlFormatter</span>().<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">format</span>(query)\n</code></pre><p>The closure is not executed against an in-memory array. The query parser reads the expression and produces an equivalent query expression, which can then be formatted for the target data provider. This gives developers a familiar JavaScript style while keeping execution in the database.</p>\n<h2>Select from a named entity</h2>\n<p><code>QueryEntity</code> represents the source entity in a query and can provide an alias:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> orders = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">QueryEntity</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;OrderData&#x27;</span>).<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">as</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;orders&#x27;</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> products = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">QueryEntity</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;ProductData&#x27;</span>).<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">as</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;orderedItem&#x27;</span>)\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> query = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">QueryExpression</span>()\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">select</span>(<span class=\"hljs-function\">(<span class=\"hljs-params\">order</span>) =&gt;</span> ({\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">id</span>: order.<span class=\"hljs-property\">id</span>,\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">orderDate</span>: order.<span class=\"hljs-property\">orderDate</span>,\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">product</span>: order.<span class=\"hljs-property\">orderedItem</span>.<span class=\"hljs-property\">name</span>,\n  }))\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">from</span>(orders)\n</code></pre><p>Aliases make joined and relational queries easier to read. They also keep generated names unambiguous when two entities contain fields with the same name.</p>\n<h2>Join related entities</h2>\n<p>A join combines rows from related entities. <code>@themost/query</code> supports inner joins as well as left and right joins:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> orders = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">QueryEntity</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;OrderData&#x27;</span>).<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">as</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;orders&#x27;</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> products = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">QueryEntity</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;ProductData&#x27;</span>).<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">as</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;orderedItem&#x27;</span>)\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> query = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">QueryExpression</span>()\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">select</span>(<span class=\"hljs-function\">(<span class=\"hljs-params\">order</span>) =&gt;</span> ({\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">id</span>: order.<span class=\"hljs-property\">id</span>,\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">orderDate</span>: order.<span class=\"hljs-property\">orderDate</span>,\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">productName</span>: order.<span class=\"hljs-property\">orderedItem</span>.<span class=\"hljs-property\">name</span>,\n  }))\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">from</span>(orders)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">join</span>(products)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">with</span>(<span class=\"hljs-function\">(<span class=\"hljs-params\">order, product</span>) =&gt;</span> order.<span class=\"hljs-property\">orderedItem</span> === product.<span class=\"hljs-property\">id</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">where</span>(<span class=\"hljs-function\">(<span class=\"hljs-params\">order</span>) =&gt;</span> order.<span class=\"hljs-property\">orderedItem</span>.<span class=\"hljs-property\">category</span> === <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Laptops&#x27;</span>)\n</code></pre><p>The join condition describes how the order and product are related. A database adapter can translate the expression into its own SQL dialect while preserving the relationship and filter.</p>\n<h2>Page and sort results</h2>\n<p>Use <code>take</code> and <code>skip</code> to create a page of results:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> query = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">QueryExpression</span>()\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">select</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;id&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;name&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;price&#x27;</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">from</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;ProductData&#x27;</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">orderBy</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;name&#x27;</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">take</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">25</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">skip</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">25</span>)\n</code></pre><p>This requests the second page when each page contains 25 records. Always pair paging with a stable order when the result is shown to a user. Without ordering, the database is free to return rows in an order that may change between requests.</p>\n<h2>Aggregate and group</h2>\n<p>The query language also supports aggregate functions such as <code>count</code>, <code>sum</code>, <code>avg</code>, <code>min</code>, and <code>max</code>:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> { <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">QueryEntity</span>, <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">QueryExpression</span>, <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">SqlFormatter</span>, count } <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;@themost/query&#x27;</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> products = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">QueryEntity</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;ProductData&#x27;</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> query = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">QueryExpression</span>()\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">select</span>(<span class=\"hljs-function\">(<span class=\"hljs-params\">product</span>) =&gt;</span> ({\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">category</span>: product.<span class=\"hljs-property\">category</span>,\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">total</span>: <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">count</span>(product.<span class=\"hljs-property\">id</span>),\n  }))\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">from</span>(products)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">groupBy</span>(<span class=\"hljs-function\">(<span class=\"hljs-params\">product</span>) =&gt;</span> product.<span class=\"hljs-property\">category</span>)\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> sql = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">SqlFormatter</span>().<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">format</span>(query)\n</code></pre><p>The result is a summary by category rather than one row for every product. Because the aggregation is part of the query expression, the database can perform the counting instead of the application loading every row first.</p>\n<h2>Query is not execution</h2>\n<p><code>@themost/query</code> builds and formats expressions. It does not need to own the application connection. A data adapter can execute the resulting expression:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> results = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">await</span> db.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">executeAsync</span>(query)\n</code></pre><p>In a model-driven application, <code>@themost/data</code> normally creates these query expressions for you when you call methods such as <code>context.model(&#39;Product&#39;).where(...)</code>. Understanding <code>@themost/query</code> helps explain what happens underneath that fluent model API.</p>\n<h2>Why database agnostic matters</h2>\n<p>A database-agnostic query is not a promise that every database has identical capabilities. It is a stable application vocabulary for common operations, with the provider-specific translation kept at the edge.</p>\n<p>That gives teams a few practical benefits:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Model and service code is less tied to one vendor&#39;s SQL dialect.</li>\n<li>Query intent is easier to read and test.</li>\n<li>Parameters can be handled by the query system instead of string concatenation.</li>\n<li>Adapters can optimize or format the same expression for different engines.</li>\n<li><code>@themost/data</code> can build model queries on top of a shared query language.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Start with <code>select</code>, <code>from</code>, <code>where</code>, <code>orderBy</code>, <code>take</code>, and <code>skip</code>. Add joins and aggregates when the domain needs them. The main habit is to describe the data operation as an expression and let the configured adapter handle the database-specific execution.</p>\n<p>For more examples, explore the <a href=\"https://github.com/themost-framework/query\"><code>@themost/query</code> repository</a> and the <a href=\"https://github.com/themost-framework/query#usage\">query module README</a>.</p>\n",
  },
  "data-dialects-and-adapters": {
    slug: "data-dialects-and-adapters",
    folder: "",
    title: "Data Dialects and Database Adapters",
    summary: "Understand how @themost-framework keeps model code portable while @themost/mysql, @themost/sqlite, @themost/pg, @themost/oracle, and other adapters handle database-specific behavior.",
    category: "Data Layer",
    readingTime: "6 min read",
    source: "data-dialects-and-adapters.md",
    contentHtml: "<h1>Data Dialects and Database Adapters</h1>\n<p>A model-driven application should not need a different business layer for every database engine. <code>@themost-framework</code> keeps models and queries on a shared API, then delegates storage details to a database adapter.</p>\n<p>This is the role of packages such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>@themost/sqlite</code> for SQLite</li>\n<li><code>@themost/mysql</code> for MySQL and MariaDB</li>\n<li><code>@themost/pg</code> for PostgreSQL</li>\n<li><code>@themost/oracle</code> for Oracle Database</li>\n<li><code>@themost/mssql</code> for Microsoft SQL Server</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Together with <code>@themost/query</code>, these adapters form a dialect boundary. The application describes a query once, and the selected adapter translates it into the syntax and execution behavior required by its database.</p>\n<h2>The shared application layer</h2>\n<p>Application code uses <code>@themost/data</code> models and query methods instead of opening a provider-specific connection in every service:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> products = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">await</span> context\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">model</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Product&#x27;</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">where</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;category&#x27;</span>).<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">equal</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Laptops&#x27;</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">where</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;price&#x27;</span>).<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">greaterThan</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">500</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">orderBy</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;name&#x27;</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">take</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">25</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">getItems</span>()\n</code></pre><p>This query expresses domain intent. It does not mention SQLite placeholders, PostgreSQL casts, MySQL identifier rules, Oracle pagination syntax, or SQL Server&#39;s paging form. The active data adapter receives the resulting query expression and handles those details.</p>\n<p>The model layer also keeps field types, relationships, constraints, privileges, and listeners consistent when the storage provider changes.</p>\n<h2>Configure an adapter type</h2>\n<p>A data configuration registers the adapter implementation by a stable invariant name:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;adapterTypes&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;SQLite Data Adapter&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;invariantName&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;sqlite&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;@themost/sqlite#SqliteAdapter&quot;</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><p>The <code>type</code> identifies the package and exported adapter class. The <code>invariantName</code> is the configuration key used by an adapter definition. This lets the rest of the application refer to a provider consistently without importing its implementation into every model or controller.</p>\n<h2>Select the active connection</h2>\n<p>An adapter definition provides the connection options and can be marked as the default:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;adapters&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;development&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;invariantName&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;sqlite&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;default&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-literal\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">true</span></span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;options&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n        <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;database&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;./db/local.db&quot;</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><p>The options depend on the provider. A SQLite adapter may need a database file path. A server database adapter commonly needs a host, port, database name, user, password, and pool settings.</p>\n<p>For example, a PostgreSQL deployment might use a configuration shaped like this:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;adapters&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;production&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;invariantName&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;postgres&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;default&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-literal\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">true</span></span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;options&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n        <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;host&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;db.example.com&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n        <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;port&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">5432</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n        <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;database&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;catalog&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n        <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;user&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;app_user&quot;</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><p>Credentials should come from environment or secret configuration in a real deployment. They should not be committed to a model or source file.</p>\n<h2>Provider packages and dialect details</h2>\n<p>Each adapter is responsible for the behavior that cannot be made identical across databases. This may include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Quoting identifiers and reserved words</li>\n<li>Parameter placeholders and value binding</li>\n<li>Date and time functions</li>\n<li>Boolean and numeric representations</li>\n<li>Auto-increment or counter columns</li>\n<li>Pagination syntax</li>\n<li>Transaction and connection-pool behavior</li>\n<li>Native data types and provider-specific features</li>\n</ul>\n<p>That does not mean every database has exactly the same capabilities. It means the framework gives common operations a shared representation and leaves provider-specific translation at the adapter boundary.</p>\n<p>For example, the same query can use <code>take(25)</code> and <code>skip(25)</code> across providers. The adapter can format that paging request according to the selected database instead of forcing model code to know whether the target engine expects <code>LIMIT</code>, <code>OFFSET</code>, <code>FETCH</code>, or another form.</p>\n<h2>Query expressions are portable too</h2>\n<p><code>@themost/query</code> builds database-independent query expressions before an adapter formats or executes them:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> { <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">QueryEntity</span>, <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">QueryExpression</span>, <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">SqlFormatter</span> } <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;@themost/query&#x27;</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> orders = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">QueryEntity</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;OrderData&#x27;</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> query = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">QueryExpression</span>()\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">select</span>(<span class=\"hljs-function\">(<span class=\"hljs-params\">order</span>) =&gt;</span> ({\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">id</span>: order.<span class=\"hljs-property\">id</span>,\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">orderDate</span>: order.<span class=\"hljs-property\">orderDate</span>,\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">status</span>: order.<span class=\"hljs-property\">orderStatus</span>,\n  }))\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">from</span>(orders)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">where</span>(<span class=\"hljs-function\">(<span class=\"hljs-params\">order</span>) =&gt;</span> order.<span class=\"hljs-property\">orderStatus</span> === <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">orderByDescending</span>(<span class=\"hljs-function\">(<span class=\"hljs-params\">order</span>) =&gt;</span> order.<span class=\"hljs-property\">orderDate</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">take</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">20</span>)\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> sql = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">SqlFormatter</span>().<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">format</span>(query)\n</code></pre><p>The formatter is useful for inspecting the generated SQL, while a configured adapter can execute the expression directly. <code>@themost/data</code> usually hides this lower-level step behind its model query API.</p>\n<h2>Moving from SQLite to PostgreSQL</h2>\n<p>SQLite is convenient for local development and tests because it is file-based and easy to start. A production system may use PostgreSQL, MySQL, Oracle, or SQL Server for operational, scaling, or organizational reasons.</p>\n<p>A provider change usually involves:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Installing the matching adapter package.</li>\n<li>Registering its adapter type and invariant name.</li>\n<li>Changing the active adapter options.</li>\n<li>Migrating or verifying the database schema and data.</li>\n<li>Running the application&#39;s model and integration tests against the new provider.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The model definitions and normal query calls should remain the same. If a change requires provider-specific logic in a model or business service, isolate it behind a deliberate adapter or service boundary rather than spreading it through the application.</p>\n<h2>Test portability honestly</h2>\n<p>Database agnostic does not mean database independent in every edge case. A query that uses a common filter and sort is likely to travel well. A query that depends on a vendor-specific function, collation, JSON behavior, or transaction feature may need a provider-specific capability check.</p>\n<p>Use a small compatibility test suite for the operations your application relies on:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Create and update model entities.</li>\n<li>Query primitive fields and relationships.</li>\n<li>Apply paging and ordering.</li>\n<li>Execute transactions and rollbacks.</li>\n<li>Validate dates, counters, null values, and unique constraints.</li>\n<li>Exercise listeners, privileges, and calculated fields.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The same model tests can run against multiple adapters. That is one of the best ways to find a portability problem while it is still local and easy to fix.</p>\n<h2>Keep the dialect at the edge</h2>\n<p>The useful design boundary is simple: domain code talks in models and query expressions, while adapters talk in database dialects. <code>@themost/sqlite</code>, <code>@themost/mysql</code>, <code>@themost/pg</code>, <code>@themost/oracle</code>, and <code>@themost/mssql</code> make that boundary concrete for their respective engines.</p>\n<p>Choose the provider that fits the deployment, configure it explicitly, and keep the application vocabulary on <code>@themost/data</code> and <code>@themost/query</code>. That way, changing the database is a controlled infrastructure decision rather than a rewrite of the business model.</p>\n<p>Explore the adapter packages through the <a href=\"https://github.com/themost-framework\"><code>@themost-framework</code> GitHub organization</a> and the <a href=\"https://github.com/themost-framework/query\"><code>@themost/query</code> documentation</a>.</p>\n",
  },
  "connection-and-data-context": {
    slug: "connection-and-data-context",
    folder: "",
    title: "Connection and Data Context",
    summary: "Understand the DataContext lifecycle and why every database operation starts from a managed connection scope.",
    category: "Core Data Layer",
    readingTime: "4 min read",
    source: "connection-and-data-context.md",
    contentHtml: "<h1>Connection and Data Context</h1>\n<p>Every database operation in <code>@themost/data</code> requires an active <code>DataContext</code>. This object manages your database connection lifecycle and acts as the central gateway to your application models.</p>\n<p>To prevent memory issues or hung socket connections, a context must always be finalized at the end of its operational execution loop.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> { <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">FreedomDataContext</span> } <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;@themost/data&#x27;</span>;\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">async</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">function</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">connectToDatabase</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\"></span>) {\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> context = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">FreedomDataContext</span>();\n\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">try</span> {\n    <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">console</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">log</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Database context initialized successfully!&#x27;</span>);\n  } <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">catch</span> (error) {\n    <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">console</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">error</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Connection scope error:&#x27;</span>, error.<span class=\"hljs-property\">message</span>);\n  } <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">finally</span> {\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">await</span> context.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">finalizeAsync</span>();\n  }\n}\n</code></pre><h2>Why it matters</h2>\n<p>The <code>DataContext</code> is the runtime boundary for your data access. It is responsible for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>opening and managing the database connection</li>\n<li>exposing model references and query APIs</li>\n<li>coordinating save and transaction behavior</li>\n<li>releasing resources cleanly when work completes</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In short, the context is the foundation on which all data operations are built.</p>\n",
  },
  "basic-data-retrieval": {
    slug: "basic-data-retrieval",
    folder: "",
    title: "Basic Data Retrieval",
    summary: "Use the fluent query API to fetch, filter, sort, and shape records with readable JavaScript patterns.",
    category: "Querying",
    readingTime: "5 min read",
    source: "basic-data-retrieval.md",
    contentHtml: "<h1>Basic Data Retrieval</h1>\n<p>Data fetching is initiated by calling <code>.asQueryable()</code> on any registered data model reference. <code>@themost/data</code> exposes a powerful, type-agnostic fluent API that resembles LINQ query patterns.</p>\n<p>This allows developers to chain clear modifiers like <code>.where()</code>, <code>.select()</code>, and <code>.orderBy()</code> to dynamically formulate execution logic.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">async</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">function</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">fetchActiveUsers</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">context</span>) {\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> users = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">await</span> context.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">model</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;User&#x27;</span>)\n    .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">asQueryable</span>()\n    .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">where</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;isActive&#x27;</span>).<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">equal</span>(<span class=\"hljs-literal\">true</span>)\n    .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">orderBy</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;lastName&#x27;</span>)\n    .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">select</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;id&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;username&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;email&#x27;</span>)\n    .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">getItems</span>();\n\n  <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">console</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">log</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Active Profiles:&#x27;</span>, users);\n}\n</code></pre><h2>Query fluency</h2>\n<p>The fluent API keeps the intent readable and close to the business logic. Instead of mixing SQL strings across the application, you express operations as a pipeline:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>filter rows</li>\n<li>add selection criteria</li>\n<li>order results</li>\n<li>fetch only what you need</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This is one of the main reasons the framework feels maintainable at scale.</p>\n",
  },
  "javascript-closures-for-filtering": {
    slug: "javascript-closures-for-filtering",
    folder: "",
    title: "JavaScript Closures for Filtering",
    summary: "Use native JavaScript predicates inside queries to express secure, readable filters without writing manual SQL.",
    category: "Querying",
    readingTime: "4 min read",
    source: "javascript-closures-for-filtering.md",
    contentHtml: "<h1>JavaScript Closures for Filtering</h1>\n<p>A standout feature of <code>@themost/data</code> is its ability to parse native JavaScript arrow functions inside a <code>.where()</code> constraint. The runtime analyzes the closure and converts it into a parameterized query plan.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">async</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">function</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">filterWithClosures</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">context</span>) {\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> premiumArticles = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">await</span> context.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">model</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Post&#x27;</span>)\n    .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">asQueryable</span>()\n    .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">where</span>(<span class=\"hljs-function\"><span class=\"hljs-params\">x</span> =&gt;</span> x.<span class=\"hljs-property\">isPremium</span> === <span class=\"hljs-literal\">true</span> &amp;&amp; x.<span class=\"hljs-property\">views</span> &gt; <span class=\"hljs-number\">1000</span> &amp;&amp; x.<span class=\"hljs-property\">title</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">includes</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Node&#x27;</span>))\n    .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">getItems</span>();\n\n  <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">console</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">log</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Filtered Premium Posts:&#x27;</span>, premiumArticles);\n}\n</code></pre><h2>Why closures are useful</h2>\n<p>This pattern keeps filtering logic close to the model and easy to read. It preserves the developer experience of writing plain JavaScript while still letting the framework translate the expression to a secure underlying query.</p>\n<p>This is a strong example of the model-first design: behavior is explicit, but storage details remain abstracted.</p>\n",
  },
  "relationships-and-eager-loading": {
    slug: "relationships-and-eager-loading",
    folder: "",
    title: "Relationships and Eager Loading",
    summary: "Load related records intentionally with expansion rules that keep data retrieval efficient and expressive.",
    category: "Relationships",
    readingTime: "5 min read",
    source: "relationships-and-eager-loading.md",
    contentHtml: "<h1>Relationships and Eager Loading</h1>\n<p>To balance network optimization with fast lookups, foreign key model relationships are evaluated lazily. When a record needs to load together with its parent or nested collections, it must be declared via the <code>.expand()</code> expression method.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">async</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">function</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">getPostsWithAuthors</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">context</span>) {\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> articles = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">await</span> context.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">model</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Post&#x27;</span>)\n    .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">asQueryable</span>()\n    .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">where</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;published&#x27;</span>).<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">equal</span>(<span class=\"hljs-literal\">true</span>)\n    .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">expand</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;author&#x27;</span>)\n    .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">getItems</span>();\n\n  <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">console</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">dir</span>(articles, { <span class=\"hljs-attr\">depth</span>: <span class=\"hljs-literal\">null</span> });\n}\n</code></pre><h2>The value of expansion</h2>\n<p>Expansion lets the application request related entities in the same data fetch. This keeps the result set coherent and reduces the need for a second query cycle.</p>\n<p>For domain-heavy apps, this is a huge win: it preserves object relationships while keeping reads efficient and predictable.</p>\n",
  },
  "atomic-transactions": {
    slug: "atomic-transactions",
    folder: "",
    title: "Atomic Transactions",
    summary: "Protect dependent data updates with transaction boundaries that roll back cleanly on failure.",
    category: "Data Integrity",
    readingTime: "4 min read",
    source: "atomic-transactions.md",
    contentHtml: "<h1>Atomic Transactions</h1>\n<p>When implementing multi-step mutation pipelines where multiple writes are dependent, wrapping instructions inside a structured transaction block is mandatory. The framework-level <code>executeInTransaction</code> loop guarantees automated rollback if any child operation fails.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">async</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">function</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">processOrderTransaction</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">context, orderPayload, itemPayload</span>) {\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">try</span> {\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">await</span> context.<span class=\"hljs-property\">db</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">executeInTransaction</span>(<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">async</span> () =&gt; {\n      <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">await</span> context.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">model</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Order&#x27;</span>).<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">save</span>(orderPayload);\n      <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">await</span> context.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">model</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;OrderItem&#x27;</span>).<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">save</span>(itemPayload);\n    });\n\n    <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">console</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">log</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Transaction safely committed to the database!&#x27;</span>);\n  } <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">catch</span> (error) {\n    <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">console</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">error</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Transaction failed. Database rolled back cleanly:&#x27;</span>, error.<span class=\"hljs-property\">message</span>);\n  }\n}\n</code></pre><h2>Why transactions matter</h2>\n<p>Transactions preserve consistency across dependent updates. If one save fails, the earlier state is rolled back so the system does not end up half-written.</p>\n<p>For business-critical flows such as order processing, payments, or record migrations, this is essential.</p>\n",
  },
  "data-listeners": {
    slug: "data-listeners",
    folder: "",
    title: "Data Listeners",
    summary: "Intercept entity flows before and after persistence to validate state and add business behavior cleanly.",
    category: "Event Model",
    readingTime: "4 min read",
    source: "data-listeners.md",
    contentHtml: "<h1>Data Listeners</h1>\n<p>Data listeners intercept entity mutation lifecycles right before or directly after records reach persistence. This makes it possible to validate input, override values, or trigger side effects during the save pipeline.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">export</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">function</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">beforeSave</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">event, callback</span>) {\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> target = event.<span class=\"hljs-property\">target</span>;\n\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">if</span> (target.<span class=\"hljs-property\">email</span> &amp;&amp; target.<span class=\"hljs-property\">email</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">endsWith</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;@dispostable.com&#x27;</span>)) {\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">callback</span>(<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">Error</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Temporary email domains are strictly blocked.&#x27;</span>));\n  }\n\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">callback</span>();\n}\n</code></pre><h2>Practical value</h2>\n<p>Listeners provide a clean place for validation and cross-cutting behavior without coupling business logic to a storage implementation. They also help keep domain rules centralized rather than scattered across controllers or service methods.</p>\n",
  },
  "aggregations-and-grouping": {
    slug: "aggregations-and-grouping",
    folder: "",
    title: "Aggregations and Grouping",
    summary: "Summarize datasets with grouping and aggregate expressions that remain readable and portable.",
    category: "Analytics",
    readingTime: "5 min read",
    source: "aggregations-and-grouping.md",
    contentHtml: "<h1>Aggregations and Grouping</h1>\n<p>When combining operational vectors for counting, summary totals, or evaluating arithmetic averages, <code>@themost/data</code> handles backend groupings with a readable query pattern. The framework binds analytical projection clauses directly into JavaScript expressions.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">async</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">function</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">getAuthorStatsReport</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">context</span>) {\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> stats = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">await</span> context.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">model</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Post&#x27;</span>)\n    .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">asQueryable</span>()\n    .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">groupBy</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;authorId&#x27;</span>)\n    .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">select</span>(\n      <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;authorId&#x27;</span>,\n      <span class=\"hljs-function\"><span class=\"hljs-params\">x</span> =&gt;</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">Object</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">assign</span>(x, {\n        <span class=\"hljs-attr\">totalViews</span>: minxs.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">sum</span>(x.<span class=\"hljs-property\">views</span>),\n        <span class=\"hljs-attr\">articleCount</span>: minxs.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">count</span>(x.<span class=\"hljs-property\">id</span>)\n      })\n    )\n    .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">getItems</span>();\n\n  <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">console</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">log</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Author Statistics:&#x27;</span>, stats);\n}\n</code></pre><h2>Why it matters</h2>\n<p>Analytics use cases often require summary data, not just record-level retrieval. Grouping and aggregation let the application compute business signals without writing custom database-specific SQL paths for every report.</p>\n",
  },
  "schema-definition-and-basic-attributes": {
    slug: "schema-definition-and-basic-attributes",
    folder: "",
    title: "Schema Definition and Basic Attributes",
    summary: "Declare models with schema metadata that defines storage mapping, constraints, and field behavior.",
    category: "Schema Design",
    readingTime: "5 min read",
    source: "schema-definition-and-basic-attributes.md",
    contentHtml: "<h1>Schema Definition and Basic Attributes</h1>\n<p>Model properties in <code>@themost/data</code> are declared using JSON-based schema configuration. These files define storage source locations, field types, sizes, indexing options, and required constraints.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Product&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;id&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">101</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;source&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Products&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;view&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ProductsView&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;fields&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;id&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Counter&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;primary&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-literal\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">true</span></span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;sku&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Text&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;size&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">50</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;nullable&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-literal\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">false</span></span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;unique&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-literal\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">true</span></span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;price&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Currency&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;nullable&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-literal\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">false</span></span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><h2>Why schema metadata matters</h2>\n<p>Schemas make the storage contract visible and maintainable. They give the framework a consistent description of the domain while allowing different backends to implement that contract in their own way.</p>\n",
  },
  "model-associations": {
    slug: "model-associations",
    folder: "",
    title: "Model Associations",
    summary: "Model one-to-many and many-to-many relationships with declarative field configuration and implicit joins.",
    category: "Relations",
    readingTime: "5 min read",
    source: "model-associations.md",
    contentHtml: "<h1>Model Associations</h1>\n<p>Declaring relational architecture fields is done within the schema definition by matching a field <code>type</code> to a target model object token. This can represent a single foreign reference or a many-to-many collection through an implicit junction relationship.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Order&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;id&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">102</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;source&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Orders&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;fields&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;id&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Counter&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;primary&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-literal\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">true</span></span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;customer&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;User&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;nullable&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-literal\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">false</span></span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;description&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Establishes a many-to-one foreign key mapping to the User model&quot;</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;tags&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Tag&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;many&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-literal\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">true</span></span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;description&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Creates an implicit many-to-many junction relationship table&quot;</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><h2>Why associations matter</h2>\n<p>Most real systems have relationships, not isolated records. Modeling associations cleanly helps keep domain logic accurate while still allowing the runtime to translate these relationships into underlying storage constructs.</p>\n",
  },
  "model-inheritance-and-polymorphism": {
    slug: "model-inheritance-and-polymorphism",
    folder: "",
    title: "Model Inheritance and Polymorphism",
    summary: "Use inheritance to extend shared model behavior while preserving specialized data contracts for child entities.",
    category: "Model Design",
    readingTime: "4 min read",
    source: "model-inheritance-and-polymorphism.md",
    contentHtml: "<h1>Model Inheritance and Data Polymorphism</h1>\n<p>Object-oriented composition mechanisms map naturally to persistence layers using the declarative <code>inherits</code> configuration parameter. A subclass definition carries all parent columns automatically while adding unique attributes in an extended storage model.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Article&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;id&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">103</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;inherits&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Post&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;source&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Articles&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;fields&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;editorNote&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Text&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;size&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">500</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;wordCount&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Integer&quot;</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><h2>Why inheritance is useful</h2>\n<p>Inheritance keeps shared behavior centralized and makes specialized records easier to maintain. This is especially important in domain models where the base object is common, but child entities need unique attributes or validation logic.</p>\n",
  },
  "data-modelling-with-themost-test": {
    slug: "data-modelling-with-themost-test",
    folder: "",
    title: "Data Modelling with @themost/test",
    summary: "Use the @themost/test API server as a practical learning environment for modelling entities, attributes, and business domains with @themost-framework.",
    category: "Data Modelling",
    readingTime: "6 min read",
    source: "data-modelling-with-themost-test.md",
    contentHtml: "<h1>Data Modelling with @themost/test</h1>\n<p>A good data model gives an application a stable vocabulary. Instead of scattering table names and storage details throughout the codebase, you describe the business objects your application understands and let the framework expose them consistently.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://github.com/themost-framework/test\"><code>@themost/test</code></a> package is a useful place to explore these ideas. It ships a runnable test API server, a temporary SQLite database, and a small CRM-style domain with customers, products, offers, and orders. You can install it without creating a complete application first:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">npm install @themost/test\nnpx @themost/test\n</code></pre><p>The server exposes the model layer through an OData v4 API. That makes the model visible both in application code and at the HTTP boundary.</p>\n<h2>Start with domain objects</h2>\n<p>The test project defines model classes under <code>modules/test/server/models</code>. A model class represents an entity type and can carry the relationships and behavior that belong to that entity. The <code>Thing</code> model is a useful base for common descriptive fields such as <code>name</code>, <code>description</code>, <code>url</code>, and audit dates:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-js\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> { <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">DataObject</span>, <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">EdmMapping</span> } <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;@themost/data&#x27;</span>\n\n@<span class=\"hljs-title class_\">EdmMapping</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">entityType</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Thing&#x27;</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">class</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">Thing</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">extends</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_ inherited__\">DataObject</span> {\n  <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">constructor</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\"></span>) {\n    <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">super</span>()\n  }\n}\n</code></pre><p>The <code>@EdmMapping.entityType()</code> decorator gives the class a public entity type name. The model can then participate in schema loading, querying, and OData metadata generation.</p>\n<p>This separation is valuable because the class describes the domain while the configured data adapter handles persistence. A caller works with a <code>Thing</code> model rather than having to know which table, connection, or SQL dialect stores it.</p>\n<h2>Reuse meaning through inheritance</h2>\n<p>The test server builds a small inheritance chain:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-text\">Thing\n  └── Account\n        └── User\n</code></pre><p><code>Account</code> extends <code>Thing</code>, and <code>User</code> extends <code>Account</code>. This means shared identity and descriptive attributes can live in one place while specialized models add only what they need. <code>User</code> can add authentication-oriented fields such as <code>enabled</code>, <code>lastLogon</code>, and <code>logonCount</code> without duplicating the base account definition.</p>\n<p>Inheritance is most useful when the relationship is semantic, not merely convenient. A <code>User</code> really is a specialized <code>Account</code>, so the hierarchy communicates something meaningful to the rest of the application. If two objects only happen to share a few columns, a reusable model definition or a relationship may be clearer than inheritance.</p>\n<h2>Let the model drive queries</h2>\n<p>Once an application has a configured <code>DataContext</code>, it can query a model by name. The query expresses what the application wants and remains independent of the underlying provider:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-js\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> users = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">await</span> context.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">model</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;User&#x27;</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">select</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;id&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;name&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;enabled&#x27;</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">where</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;enabled&#x27;</span>).<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">equal</span>(<span class=\"hljs-literal\">true</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">orderBy</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;name&#x27;</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">getItems</span>()\n</code></pre><p>The important detail is that the query starts from the model and its attributes. It does not need to embed a table name or manually assemble a SQL statement. That gives the application a consistent query language as the domain grows and makes provider changes less invasive.</p>\n<h2>A model is a contract</h2>\n<p>A model should answer a few practical questions clearly:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>What does this entity represent?</li>\n<li>Which attributes are required to identify and describe it?</li>\n<li>Which other entities does it relate to?</li>\n<li>Which rules belong to the entity or its lifecycle?</li>\n<li>Which parts should be shared with a base model?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <code>@themost/test</code> project is deliberately approachable because its model classes, configuration, and database can be inspected together. Start with the model definitions, run the server, and compare the code with the generated API metadata. That feedback loop turns modelling from an abstract exercise into something you can query and verify.</p>\n<h2>Next steps</h2>\n<p>Run the test API server and open its schema documentation at <code>/api/docs</code>. The generated model list shows entity types and their inheritance relationships. You can also request the machine-readable metadata document at <code>/api/$metadata</code>.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://github.com/themost-framework/test\">@themost/test repository</a> is a practical companion to the framework: it provides a known domain, real queries, and a working API surface while you learn how models become application behavior.</p>\n",
  },
  "model-inheritance-and-relationships": {
    slug: "model-inheritance-and-relationships",
    folder: "",
    title: "Model Inheritance and Relationships",
    summary: "See how @themost/test connects entity types with inheritance and relationships, then exposes the model as queryable OData metadata.",
    category: "Data Modelling",
    readingTime: "6 min read",
    source: "model-inheritance-and-relationships.md",
    contentHtml: "<h1>Model Inheritance and Relationships</h1>\n<p>A domain becomes useful when its entities can describe how things fit together. Inheritance captures shared meaning between types. Relationships connect separate entities so an application can navigate the domain without rebuilding joins by hand.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://github.com/themost-framework/test\"><code>@themost/test</code></a> project includes both patterns in a small, inspectable application. Its model classes use <code>@themost/data</code> mappings, while the test API server publishes the resulting model as an OData v4 service.</p>\n<h2>Inheritance describes specialization</h2>\n<p>The sample models use a straightforward hierarchy:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-js\">@<span class=\"hljs-title class_\">EdmMapping</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">entityType</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Account&#x27;</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">class</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">Account</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">extends</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_ inherited__\">Thing</span> {\n  <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">constructor</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\"></span>) {\n    <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">super</span>()\n  }\n}\n\n@<span class=\"hljs-title class_\">EdmMapping</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">entityType</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;User&#x27;</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">class</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">User</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">extends</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_ inherited__\">Account</span> {\n  <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">constructor</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\"></span>) {\n    <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">super</span>()\n  }\n}\n</code></pre><p><code>Account</code> inherits the common properties of <code>Thing</code>. <code>User</code> then specializes <code>Account</code> with user-specific state and behavior, including a <code>getMe()</code> function and credential validation in the test server.</p>\n<p>This gives consumers a coherent contract. A user can be treated as an account when shared behavior is enough, or as a user when the application needs authentication and group membership. The model hierarchy carries that distinction into generated metadata and documentation.</p>\n<h2>Relationships make navigation explicit</h2>\n<p>The sample also models membership relationships. A <code>User</code> exposes a <code>groups</code> collection, and a <code>Group</code> exposes its <code>members</code>. These properties describe the domain directly:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-js\">@<span class=\"hljs-title class_\">EdmMapping</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">entityType</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;User&#x27;</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">class</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">User</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">extends</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_ inherited__\">Account</span> {\n  <span class=\"hljs-comment\">// groups: Group[]</span>\n}\n\n@<span class=\"hljs-title class_\">EdmMapping</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">entityType</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Group&#x27;</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">class</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">Group</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">extends</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_ inherited__\">Account</span> {\n  <span class=\"hljs-comment\">// members: Account[]</span>\n}\n</code></pre><p>The exact storage representation can remain an implementation detail. At the model boundary, callers work with meaningful names such as <code>groups</code> and <code>members</code>. This is easier to understand than carrying foreign-key conventions through every part of an application.</p>\n<p>Relationships also give the query layer enough information to support related data retrieval. For example, an application can request a user and choose whether to include related groups, depending on what the screen or workflow needs. Keeping that choice in the query avoids loading every relationship by default.</p>\n<h2>Inspect the generated contract</h2>\n<p>When <code>@themost/test</code> starts, it creates an <code>ExpressDataApplication</code> and exposes the model layer through <code>/api/</code>. The server provides a metadata document at <code>/api/$metadata</code> and browser-friendly schema documentation at <code>/api/docs</code>.</p>\n<p>You can inspect the metadata with curl:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">curl http://localhost:3000/api/\\<span class=\"hljs-variable\">$metadata</span> \\\n  -H <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Accept: application/xml&#x27;</span>\n</code></pre><p>The document contains entity types and entity sets derived from the configured models. It is a useful modelling check: if an entity, inherited attribute, or relationship is not represented as expected, the model configuration should be corrected before client code is built around it.</p>\n<p>The test server&#39;s documentation route also lists model inheritance. Its model view displays whether a type inherits from or implements another type, which makes the structure easy to verify without reading every source file.</p>\n<h2>Query related data at the API boundary</h2>\n<p>The same model contract is available to API consumers through OData query options. A simple product request from the test project looks like this:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">curl <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;http://localhost:3000/api/Products?%24select=category%2Cprice%2Cmodel%2Cname&#x27;</span> \\\n  -H <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Authorization: Bearer &lt;access-token&gt;&#x27;</span>\n</code></pre><p>The client asks for fields from the <code>Product</code> model, not columns from a particular SQLite table. That distinction is what lets the framework keep domain naming stable while adapters and storage evolve underneath it.</p>\n<h2>Keep relationships purposeful</h2>\n<p>A relationship should describe a real business connection and have a clear direction. Before adding one, ask:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Is this a one-to-one, one-to-many, or many-to-many concept?</li>\n<li>Which side owns the lifecycle or association?</li>\n<li>Should the related collection be loaded for every use case, or only on demand?</li>\n<li>Does the relationship name read naturally in application code?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <code>User</code> and <code>Group</code> example is useful because it represents a real membership concept. It also demonstrates why a model is more than a list of fields: it is a navigable description of the domain.</p>\n<h2>Model first, storage second</h2>\n<p>Inheritance and relationships are most effective when they are designed from the business vocabulary outward. Start with the entities and connections that users recognize, then map those concepts to storage and API behavior. With <code>@themost/test</code>, you can run that model immediately, inspect its metadata, and exercise it through real queries.</p>\n<p>For more examples, explore the <a href=\"https://github.com/themost-framework/test/tree/master/modules/test/server/models\"><code>server/models</code></a> directory and the <a href=\"https://github.com/themost-framework/test/blob/master/README.md\"><code>@themost/test</code> README</a>. They show how a model-driven application can serve both as a production architecture and as a dependable test fixture.</p>\n",
  },
  "order-as-a-multi-relational-model": {
    slug: "order-as-a-multi-relational-model",
    folder: "",
    title: "Order as a Multi-Relational Model",
    summary: "Use the Order schema from @themost/test to understand how one business object can connect customers, offers, products, payments, status, and access rules.",
    category: "Data Modelling",
    readingTime: "7 min read",
    source: "order-as-a-multi-relational-model.md",
    contentHtml: "<h1>Order as a Multi-Relational Model</h1>\n<p>An order looks simple in a user interface, but it represents a network of business facts. Someone placed it, a merchant accepted it, a product was ordered, an offer may have determined its price, payment is due, delivery has a status, and the customer should only see the orders they are allowed to access.</p>\n<p>The <code>Order</code> schema in <a href=\"https://github.com/themost-framework/test/blob/master/modules/test/server/config/models/Order.json\"><code>@themost/test</code></a> is a useful example of this kind of multi-relational model. It shows how <code>@themost-framework</code> can keep the business meaning visible while the database remains an implementation detail.</p>\n<h2>The order is the centre of a graph</h2>\n<p>The schema starts with an identifier and transaction details, then connects the order to other models through typed fields:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Order&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;fields&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;customer&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Person&quot;</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;merchant&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Party&quot;</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;acceptedOffer&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Offer&quot;</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;orderedItem&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Product&quot;</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;orderStatus&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;OrderStatusType&quot;</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;billingAddress&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;PostalAddress&quot;</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;paymentMethod&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;PaymentMethod&quot;</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><p>Each relationship answers a different business question:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>customer</code> identifies the person who placed the order.</li>\n<li><code>merchant</code> identifies the party taking the order.</li>\n<li><code>acceptedOffer</code> records the commercial offer that was accepted.</li>\n<li><code>orderedItem</code> identifies the product being purchased.</li>\n<li><code>orderStatus</code> describes the current point in the order lifecycle.</li>\n<li><code>billingAddress</code> and <code>paymentMethod</code> describe how the transaction is settled.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These are not interchangeable references. Keeping them as separate typed properties prevents an application from collapsing different meanings into a generic <code>relatedId</code> or a collection of opaque foreign keys.</p>\n<h2>Follow relationships to their own models</h2>\n<p>The graph continues beyond the immediate fields. <code>Offer</code> inherits from <code>Intangible</code> and includes its own relationships to a <code>Person</code> seller, an <code>Organization</code> or person offering it, and the <code>Product</code> being offered:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Offer&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;inherits&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Intangible&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;fields&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;price&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Number&quot;</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;validFrom&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;DateTime&quot;</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;validThrough&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;DateTime&quot;</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;seller&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Person&quot;</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;offeredBy&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Organization&quot;</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;itemOffered&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Product&quot;</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><p>That means an order can preserve more than a product reference. It can preserve the accepted commercial context: which offer was selected, its price, when it was valid, who supplied it, and which product it described.</p>\n<p>This distinction matters when prices, promotions, or sellers change later. A query for the current product should not be confused with the offer that was accepted when the order was placed.</p>\n<h2>Model both directions when navigation matters</h2>\n<p>The <code>Person</code> schema contains an <code>orders</code> field with an association mapping back to <code>Order.customer</code>:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;orders&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Order&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;mapping&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;associationType&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;association&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;childModel&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Order&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;childField&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;customer&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;parentModel&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Person&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;parentField&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;id&quot;</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><p>This is the two-sided view of the same relationship. From an order, the application can navigate to its <code>customer</code>. From a person, it can navigate to their <code>orders</code>. The mapping tells the framework how those two model properties correspond without requiring every caller to write the join manually.</p>\n<p>A useful rule is to expose both directions only when the application has a real use for both. Customer history needs <code>Person.orders</code>; checkout and authorization need <code>Order.customer</code>. A relationship should support a workflow, not merely mirror every database link.</p>\n<h2>Keep lifecycle state as a model</h2>\n<p><code>orderStatus</code> is typed as <code>OrderStatusType</code> rather than stored as an unexplained number. The schema provides a default status object with the alternate name <code>OrderProcessing</code>, while the status model can represent other lifecycle states such as delivered or returned.</p>\n<p>That makes status usable at the domain boundary:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-js\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> latestOrders = <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">await</span> context.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">model</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Order&#x27;</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">where</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;orderStatus&#x27;</span>).<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">equal</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;OrderProcessing&#x27;</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">orderByDescending</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;orderDate&#x27;</span>)\n  .<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">getItems</span>()\n</code></pre><p>The important idea is not the exact query syntax. It is that the application can talk about order states by model vocabulary instead of spreading magic values through controllers and views.</p>\n<h2>Turn common relationships into views</h2>\n<p>The <code>Order</code> schema defines two named views:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;views&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;delivered&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;title&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Delivered Orders&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;filter&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;orderStatus eq 1&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;order&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;dateCreated desc&quot;</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;latest&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;title&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Latest Orders&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;filter&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;orderDate gt lastMonth()&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;order&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;dateCreated desc&quot;</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]</span>\n</code></pre><p>A view gives a repeated business query a stable name. The application can ask for the latest orders without duplicating its date filter and sort order in every endpoint. If the definition of “latest” changes, the model configuration is the natural place to update it.</p>\n<p>Views are especially valuable for multi-relational models because they provide a safe starting point for common workflows. A delivered-orders view can later be expanded with related customer or product data without changing the meaning of the view itself.</p>\n<h2>Access rules belong near the model</h2>\n<p>The schema also describes privileges. Global access is granted to configured accounts, while the self-level rule limits a user to orders where <code>customer/user eq me()</code>.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;mask&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;self&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;filter&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;customer/user eq me()&quot;</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><p>This is an important modelling boundary. The <code>Order</code> model knows what it means for an order to belong to a customer, so the access rule can follow the same relationship used by the rest of the application. Authorization does not need to reconstruct ownership through unrelated request parameters.</p>\n<h2>Query the graph deliberately</h2>\n<p>A client can select order fields through the OData API and expand related models when it needs their details. For example, an order summary might request the order number, date, customer, product, and status while leaving payment details out of the response.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-text\">/api/Orders?$select=orderNumber,orderDate,customer,orderedItem,orderStatus\n</code></pre><p>The model makes the graph available; the query decides how much of it to retrieve. That keeps responses focused and avoids loading every relationship for every screen.</p>\n<p>Start the learning server with:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">npx @themost/test\n</code></pre><p>Then inspect the generated schema at <code>/api/docs</code> or <code>/api/$metadata</code>. The documentation reveals the entity types and their attributes, while the metadata endpoint provides the machine-readable contract that clients can use.</p>\n<h2>A model, not a table</h2>\n<p>The <code>Order</code> schema demonstrates the central promise of model-first design. An order is not just a row with a status column. It is a relationship between a customer, merchant, offer, product, address, payment method, and lifecycle state, with reusable views and access rules attached to the same business definition.</p>\n<p>By describing that graph in the model layer, <code>@themost-framework</code> gives application code a durable vocabulary. Storage adapters, API routes, and clients can evolve around the model without each one inventing a different interpretation of what an order means.</p>\n",
  },
  "primitive-types-in-model-schemas": {
    slug: "primitive-types-in-model-schemas",
    folder: "",
    title: "Primitive Types in Model Schemas",
    summary: "Learn how @themost-framework primitive types such as Text, Number, Counter, Boolean, and Date describe the fields in a model.",
    category: "Schema Design",
    readingTime: "4 min read",
    source: "primitive-types-in-model-schemas.md",
    contentHtml: "<h1>Primitive Types in Model Schemas</h1>\n<p>A model schema describes the shape of an entity. Every field has a <code>name</code> and a <code>type</code>, and the type tells <code>@themost-framework</code> what kind of value the field stores and how it should be handled.</p>\n<p>Here is a small model with several common primitive types:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Product&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;fields&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;id&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Counter&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;primary&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-literal\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">true</span></span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Text&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;size&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">120</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;nullable&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-literal\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">false</span></span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;price&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Number&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;nullable&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-literal\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">false</span></span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;available&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Boolean&quot;</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><h2>Text</h2>\n<p><code>Text</code> stores a string value such as a product name, order number, description, or email address.</p>\n<p>Use <code>size</code> when the field has a known maximum length. Use <code>nullable: false</code> when the model requires a value:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;sku&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Text&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;size&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">50</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;nullable&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-literal\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">false</span></span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;unique&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-literal\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">true</span></span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><p>The constraints stay with the model definition, so validation and storage mapping can use the same contract.</p>\n<h2>Number</h2>\n<p><code>Number</code> stores numeric values such as prices, quantities, ratings, or measurements:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;quantity&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Number&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;nullable&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-literal\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">false</span></span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><p>Use a numeric type for values that will be calculated or compared. A price should not be stored as <code>Text</code>, because sorting the strings <code>&quot;9.50&quot;</code> and <code>&quot;100.00&quot;</code> would not have the same meaning as sorting numeric values.</p>\n<p>For values that represent money, the schema can use the more specific <code>Currency</code> type. The type communicates the intention of the field to both the framework and the people reading the model.</p>\n<h2>Counter</h2>\n<p><code>Counter</code> is commonly used for an automatically generated identifier:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;id&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Counter&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;primary&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-literal\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">true</span></span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><p>A counter is useful when the storage provider should assign a new numeric key as records are created. Application code can create a new entity without inventing an identifier first.</p>\n<p>A primary counter identifies the record. It is different from a business number such as an order number, which may have its own formatting, visibility, and generation rules.</p>\n<h2>Integer</h2>\n<p><code>Integer</code> describes a whole number without a fractional part. It is useful for counts, flags represented as numbers, or references that are intentionally numeric:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;sortOrder&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Integer&quot;</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><p>Choose <code>Integer</code> when fractions do not make sense. Choose <code>Number</code> when a value may contain a decimal part or when the domain calls for general numeric behavior.</p>\n<h2>Boolean</h2>\n<p><code>Boolean</code> stores a true-or-false value:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;enabled&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Boolean&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;nullable&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-literal\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">false</span></span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><p>Boolean fields are a good fit for switches such as <code>enabled</code>, <code>isGift</code>, or <code>isArchived</code>. If a field must always have a decision, make it non-nullable so the model does not have to interpret a missing value.</p>\n<h2>Date and DateTime</h2>\n<p>Use <code>Date</code> or <code>DateTime</code> for temporal values. Typical examples include creation dates, order dates, due dates, and the moment an entity was last modified:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;orderDate&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;DateTime&quot;</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><p>A date-only field represents a calendar date. A date-time field represents a point in time and is appropriate when time and ordering matter. Keeping temporal values typed also lets queries compare them as dates instead of treating them as arbitrary strings.</p>\n<h2>Primitive fields and model relationships</h2>\n<p>Not every field points to a primitive value. A field whose type is another model describes a relationship:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;customer&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Person&quot;</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><p><code>customer</code> is not a string containing a person name. It connects <code>Order</code> to the <code>Person</code> model. The distinction matters because related models can have their own fields, rules, and relationships.</p>\n<p>A quick way to recognize the difference is:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>Text</code>, <code>Number</code>, <code>Integer</code>, <code>Boolean</code>, <code>Date</code>, and <code>DateTime</code> describe values.</li>\n<li><code>Counter</code> commonly describes a generated identity value.</li>\n<li><code>Currency</code> describes a monetary value.</li>\n<li>A type such as <code>Person</code>, <code>Product</code>, or <code>OrderStatusType</code> describes a related model or domain type.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Start with the domain value</h2>\n<p>When adding a field, choose the type that best describes the business meaning first. Then add constraints such as <code>nullable</code>, <code>size</code>, <code>unique</code>, <code>primary</code>, or a calculated <code>value</code> where the model needs them.</p>\n<p>A clear schema gives the framework a shared contract for storage, validation, querying, and API metadata. Small type choices such as <code>Text</code> versus <code>Number</code> become valuable because they keep the model honest as the application grows.</p>\n",
  },
  "data-privileges": {
    slug: "data-privileges",
    folder: "",
    title: "Data Privileges",
    summary: "Learn the basics of model-level access rules in @themost/data using global, account, and self privileges.",
    category: "Security",
    readingTime: "4 min read",
    source: "data-privileges.md",
    contentHtml: "<h1>Data Privileges</h1>\n<p>A model should describe not only what data looks like, but also who can access it. In <code>@themost/data</code>, privileges are declared in the model schema and define which users or accounts can perform operations on an entity.</p>\n<p>A simple privilege definition looks like this:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;privileges&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;mask&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">15</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;global&quot;</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><p>Keeping access rules near the model makes them easier to understand and reuse. API routes, background jobs, and other callers can use the same model contract instead of each implementing a different permission check.</p>\n<h2>The privilege mask</h2>\n<p>The <code>mask</code> value describes the operations granted by the privilege. It is represented as a number so several operation permissions can be combined in one rule.</p>\n<p>The exact mask values depend on the framework&#39;s permission definitions, but the important starting point is simple: a larger combined mask can grant several operations, while a smaller mask can grant a narrower capability. Existing schemas commonly use <code>15</code> for broad administrative access and <code>1</code> for a restricted rule.</p>\n<p>Treat the mask as part of the schema contract. Prefer the framework&#39;s named permission constants or the conventions used by your application when defining new rules, rather than scattering unexplained numbers through application code.</p>\n<h2>Global access</h2>\n<p>A <code>global</code> privilege applies across the model without limiting access to a particular account or record:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;mask&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">15</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;global&quot;</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><p>This is useful for system-wide defaults. For example, a model might allow general access to authenticated users while using a more specific account or self rule for sensitive operations.</p>\n<p>Global does not necessarily mean anonymous. Authentication and the model&#39;s privilege rules work together. The application still decides whether a request has an authenticated user and then evaluates which privileges that user can use.</p>\n<h2>Account-specific access</h2>\n<p>A privilege can target an account by including the <code>account</code> property:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;mask&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">15</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;global&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;account&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Administrators&quot;</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><p>This grants the specified operations to members of the <code>Administrators</code> account. Account-based rules are useful for roles such as administrators, editors, support agents, or managers.</p>\n<p>The account name is part of your application&#39;s authorization vocabulary. Keep those names consistent with the accounts configured in the application, and avoid relying on a role name that exists only in one controller or route.</p>\n<h2>Self-level access</h2>\n<p>A <code>self</code> privilege limits access to records related to the current user:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;mask&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;self&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;filter&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;customer/user eq me()&quot;</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><p>This pattern appears in the <code>Order</code> schema from <a href=\"https://github.com/themost-framework/test/blob/master/modules/test/server/config/models/Order.json\"><code>@themost/test</code></a>. It says that the current user can access orders whose customer is connected to that user.</p>\n<p>The <code>filter</code> is evaluated using the model&#39;s fields and relationships. Because it follows the <code>customer</code> relationship, the rule remains aligned with the domain model instead of trusting an arbitrary customer identifier supplied by the request.</p>\n<h2>Combine rules carefully</h2>\n<p>A model can define multiple privileges for different audiences:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;privileges&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;mask&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">15</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;global&quot;</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;mask&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">15</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;global&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;account&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Administrators&quot;</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;mask&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;self&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;filter&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;customer/user eq me()&quot;</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><p>Read this as a set of access rules, not as application prose. Each entry has a target, a scope, and an operation mask. When a rule becomes difficult to explain, split it into smaller rules or give the model a clearer relationship to follow.</p>\n<h2>Privileges and relationships</h2>\n<p>Privileges become especially useful on relational models. An <code>Order</code> is connected to a <code>Person</code> through <code>customer</code>, so the order&#39;s self rule can follow that association. Similar rules can protect documents owned by a user, tasks assigned to a user, or messages addressed to a user.</p>\n<p>The relationship must be part of the model contract. If a filter depends on a field that is missing, ambiguously named, or mapped differently across models, the access rule will be difficult to maintain and verify.</p>\n<h2>Start with least privilege</h2>\n<p>When designing a new model, begin with the smallest access required by each user group. Then add broader account privileges only where the workflow needs them. Test both allowed and denied cases, including a user who owns a record, a user who does not own it, and an administrator.</p>\n<p>Privileges are one part of the security design. They should be combined with authentication, input validation, and careful response shaping. Their strength is that the model can express the data ownership rules once, close to the relationships and fields that define the domain.</p>\n<p>For a working example, start the <code>@themost/test</code> server and inspect the model documentation at <code>/api/docs</code>. The <code>Order</code> schema shows how privileges, relationships, views, and business fields can live together in one readable definition.</p>\n",
  },
  "model-event-listeners": {
    slug: "model-event-listeners",
    folder: "",
    title: "Model Event Listeners",
    summary: "Learn how @themost/data model eventListeners run during save, remove, query, and upgrade operations.",
    category: "Event Model",
    readingTime: "5 min read",
    source: "model-event-listeners.md",
    contentHtml: "<h1>Model Event Listeners</h1>\n<p>A model event listener is a small module that runs when something happens to a data model. It can validate an object before saving, react after a new record is created, prevent a delete, or observe a query before it reaches the data provider.</p>\n<p>Listeners are useful when a rule belongs to the model and should run no matter which part of the application performs the operation. A web controller, a background job, and a test can all use the same model lifecycle.</p>\n<h2>Register a listener in the schema</h2>\n<p>A model declares its listeners with the <code>eventListeners</code> property. Each entry points to a module and may include a readable name:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-json\"><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Order&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;eventListeners&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;New Order Notification&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;</span><span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;/app/controllers/new-order-listener&quot;</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}</span>\n</code></pre><p>When the model is loaded, <code>@themost/data</code> resolves the module and registers any supported listener methods it exports. A listener can also refer to a particular exported class or object with a <code>#</code> suffix, such as <code>./listeners/OnUpdateProduct#OnUpdateProduct</code>.</p>\n<h2>Write a before-save listener</h2>\n<p>A listener method receives two arguments: an event object and a callback. The event contains information about the operation, including the target object and the model. The callback tells the framework that the listener has finished:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">export</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">function</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">beforeSave</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">event, callback</span>) {\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">const</span> order = event.<span class=\"hljs-property\">target</span>;\n\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">if</span> (!order.<span class=\"hljs-property\">orderNumber</span>) {\n    order.<span class=\"hljs-property\">orderNumber</span> = <span class=\"hljs-string\">`ORDER-<span class=\"hljs-subst\">${<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">Date</span>.now()}</span>`</span>;\n  }\n\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">callback</span>();\n}\n</code></pre><p>A <code>beforeSave</code> listener runs before the object is persisted. It can normalize values, calculate a field, or reject invalid state. To stop the operation, pass an error to the callback:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">export</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">function</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">beforeSave</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">event, callback</span>) {\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">if</span> (event.<span class=\"hljs-property\">target</span>.<span class=\"hljs-property\">total</span> &lt; <span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>) {\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">callback</span>(<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">Error</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Order total cannot be negative.&#x27;</span>));\n  }\n\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">callback</span>();\n}\n</code></pre><p>The callback is important. If the listener does not call it, the save pipeline cannot continue or finish correctly.</p>\n<h2>React after a save</h2>\n<p>An <code>afterSave</code> listener runs after an object has been created or updated. It is a good place for side effects such as sending a notification, writing an audit entry, or updating an external system:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">export</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">function</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">afterSave</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">event, callback</span>) {\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">if</span> (event.<span class=\"hljs-property\">state</span> !== <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>) {\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">callback</span>();\n  }\n\n  <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">console</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">log</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;A new order was created:&#x27;</span>, event.<span class=\"hljs-property\">target</span>.<span class=\"hljs-property\">orderNumber</span>);\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">callback</span>();\n}\n</code></pre><p>The <code>state</code> value tells the listener what kind of save occurred. Check it before performing an action that should happen only for a new record. A notification that belongs only to creation should not be sent again every time the order is edited.</p>\n<p>External work should be handled carefully. If a notification is essential to the business operation, decide whether an error should fail the save. If it is optional, log the failure and keep the persistence workflow reliable according to the application&#39;s requirements.</p>\n<h2>Other lifecycle methods</h2>\n<p>A listener module can implement more than one lifecycle method:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">export</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">function</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">beforeRemove</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">event, callback</span>) {\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">if</span> (event.<span class=\"hljs-property\">target</span>.<span class=\"hljs-property\">isProtected</span>) {\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">callback</span>(<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">Error</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;This record cannot be removed.&#x27;</span>));\n  }\n\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">callback</span>();\n}\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">export</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">function</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">afterRemove</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">event, callback</span>) {\n  <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">console</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">log</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Removed:&#x27;</span>, event.<span class=\"hljs-property\">target</span>.<span class=\"hljs-property\">id</span>);\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">callback</span>();\n}\n</code></pre><p>Common model events include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>beforeSave</code>: validate or prepare an object before insert or update.</li>\n<li><code>afterSave</code>: react after insert or update completes.</li>\n<li><code>beforeRemove</code>: prevent or prepare a delete.</li>\n<li><code>afterRemove</code>: react after deletion completes.</li>\n<li><code>beforeExecute</code>: inspect or change a query before execution.</li>\n<li><code>afterExecute</code>: inspect the result after a query executes.</li>\n<li><code>beforeUpgrade</code> and <code>afterUpgrade</code>: respond when a model is upgraded or migrated.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The framework only registers methods that the listener exports. A module can therefore focus on the events it actually needs.</p>\n<h2>Understand the event object</h2>\n<p>The event argument is a <code>DataEventArgs</code> object. Its most useful properties are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>model</code>: the <code>DataModel</code> that raised the event.</li>\n<li><code>target</code>: the data object being inserted, updated, or removed.</li>\n<li><code>state</code>: the operation state, such as insert, update, or delete.</li>\n<li><code>previous</code>: the previous version of the object when it is available.</li>\n<li><code>emitter</code>: the query or event emitter involved in the operation.</li>\n<li><code>query</code>: the underlying query expression when the event concerns execution.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For example, an update listener can compare the current and previous values:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-javascript\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">export</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">function</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">beforeSave</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">event, callback</span>) {\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">if</span> (event.<span class=\"hljs-property\">state</span> === <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span> &amp;&amp; event.<span class=\"hljs-property\">previous</span>) {\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">if</span> (event.<span class=\"hljs-property\">previous</span>.<span class=\"hljs-property\">status</span> !== event.<span class=\"hljs-property\">target</span>.<span class=\"hljs-property\">status</span>) {\n      <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">console</span>.<span class=\"hljs-title function_\">log</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">`Status changed on <span class=\"hljs-subst\">${event.model.name}</span>`</span>);\n    }\n  }\n\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">callback</span>();\n}\n</code></pre><p>Use the event information instead of reading request-specific variables from inside the listener. That keeps the listener reusable wherever the model is used.</p>\n<h2>Keep listeners focused</h2>\n<p>A listener should enforce one model rule or coordinate one small piece of lifecycle behavior. Good listeners are predictable, finish quickly, and always call their callback.</p>\n<p>Avoid putting an entire application workflow inside a single listener. If several operations are involved, call a dedicated service from the listener and keep the model event method responsible for translating lifecycle events into that service call.</p>\n<p>Also remember that listeners may run for every operation on the model. A slow network request in <code>beforeSave</code>, for example, can slow every save. Choose <code>before</code> or <code>after</code> based on whether the operation must be blocked by the listener&#39;s work.</p>\n<h2>The listener lifecycle</h2>\n<p>The basic flow looks like this:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"hljs language-text\">application calls model.save(object)\n        |\n        v\nbeforeSave listeners run\n        |\n        v\nobject is persisted, or an error stops the operation\n        |\n        v\nafterSave listeners run\n</code></pre><p>This is the main idea behind model event listeners: the model owns the lifecycle, while each listener contributes one focused rule or reaction. Once the listener calls its callback, the framework can move to the next listener or finish the operation.</p>\n<p>Start with a <code>beforeSave</code> validation rule, test the allowed and rejected cases, and then add an <code>afterSave</code> reaction when the persisted change needs to notify another part of the system.</p>\n",
  }
}

